const O={};globalThis.P=(en,ar)=>({en,ar});try{globalThis["D"]=O["D"]=({"sites":[{"id":"hili","n":"Hili Archaeological Park (Al Ain)","na":"حديقة الهيلي الأثرية (العين)","c":"archaeological","s":"Bronze Age oasis village · World Heritage","lat":24.2929,"lng":55.7899,"p":"Umm an-Nar to Iron Age","dr":"c. 3000 – 300 BC","el":"Grand Tomb 12 m across","ic":"mound","era":"P","t0":5025,"t1":2325,"te":"The first farmers of the oasis","ta":"أوائل مزارعي الواحة","se":"Wheat, barley and dates were grown here 5,000 years ago beside a tomb for 600 people","sa":"زُرع القمح والشعير والتمر هنا قبل 5,000 عام إلى جانب قبر لستمئة إنسان","desc":"The Hili 8 settlement, dating to about 3000 BC, holds some of the earliest evidence of oasis agriculture in south-eastern Arabia (wheat, barley and date palm). The reconstructed Hili Grand Tomb of the Umm an-Nar culture is about 12 m in diameter and was originally about 4 m high; Tomb N, from the last two centuries of the 3rd millennium BC, held more than 600 burials. Aflaj irrigation channels dug in the first millennium BC are among the oldest known, and 3,000-year-old fingerprints of wall-builders were reported at Hili II in 2019. Part of the Cultural Sites of Al Ain, 17 locations inscribed in 2011 under criteria (iii), (iv) and (v), the UAE's first World Heritage property.","sig":"The clearest evidence of how farming, tower tombs and falaj irrigation began in the oases of eastern Arabia.","ex":"Danish, Iraqi and French missions from the 1960s; Department of Culture and Tourism – Abu Dhabi","disc":["Earliest oasis farming in south-east Arabia, c. 3000 BC","Tomb N with over 600 burials","Iron Age aflaj among the oldest known"],"refs":["UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Cultural Sites of Al Ain (Hafit, Hili, Bidaa Bint Saud and Oases Areas), ref. 1343, inscribed 2011.","Emirates Natural History Group (2011). The United Arab Emirates throughout the ages.","Potts, D. T. (2003). Archaeology of the United Arab Emirates. Trident Press."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"jebel-hafeet-tombs","n":"Jebel Hafeet Tombs","na":"مدافن جبل حفيت","c":"archaeological","s":"Beehive tombs of the Hafit period","lat":24.07,"lng":55.78,"p":"Hafit period (Early Bronze Age)","dr":"c. 3200 – 2600 BC","el":"More than 500 cairns on the foothills near Mezyad","ic":"mound","era":"P","t0":5225,"t1":4625,"te":"Five hundred stone beehives","ta":"خمسمئة خلية نحل من حجر","se":"Tombs on the mountain foothills hold pottery carried from Mesopotamia 5,000 years ago","sa":"قبور على سفوح الجبل تحوي فخارًا جُلب من بلاد الرافدين قبل 5,000 عام","desc":"More than 500 stone cairns on the foothills of Jebel Hafeet near Mezyad, first discovered in 1950 and excavated from 1962 by the Danish expedition. They date to 3200–2600 BC and give their name to the Hafit period, the start of the Bronze Age in the UAE. Imported Mesopotamian pottery of Jemdet Nasr type, about 5,000 years old, shows contact with the cities of Iraq. Part of the Cultural Sites of Al Ain, inscribed in 2011.","sig":"The earliest monumental tombs in the Emirates and the first evidence of long-distance trade with Mesopotamia.","ex":"Danish Archaeological Expedition, 1962; Department of Culture and Tourism – Abu Dhabi","disc":["Over 500 beehive cairns","Jemdet Nasr pottery from Mesopotamia"],"refs":["Emirates Natural History Group (2011). The United Arab Emirates throughout the ages.","UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Cultural Sites of Al Ain, ref. 1343."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"faya-palaeolandscape","n":"Faya Palaeolandscape","na":"منظر الفاية القديم","c":"archaeological","s":"210,000 years of human presence · World Heritage","lat":25.12,"lng":55.83,"p":"Palaeolithic to Neolithic","dr":"c. 210,000 years ago onwards","el":"18 archaeological layers","ic":"eye","era":"P","t0":210000,"t1":5000,"te":"The desert that kept people alive","ta":"الصحراء التي أبقت الناس أحياء","se":"Eighteen layers of stone tools show humans returning here for 210,000 years","sa":"ثماني عشرة طبقة من الأدوات الحجرية تُظهر عودة الإنسان إلى هنا طوال 210,000 عام","desc":"A desert landscape of dunes, rocky outcrops and fossil remnants around Jebel Faya in central Sharjah, where 30 years of excavation by the Sharjah Archaeology Authority and international teams have uncovered 18 distinct layers of human activity spanning more than 210,000 years. A joint German–Emirati team led by Hans-Peter Uerpmann found handaxes, blades and points about 125,000 years old in 2011. Inscribed on the World Heritage List in 2025 as a cultural landscape, the first desert Palaeolithic site on the list and the only Arab-region site inscribed that year.","sig":"A key site for how early humans crossed and survived Arabia during the wet and dry phases of the last 200,000 years.","ex":"Sharjah Archaeology Authority with the University of Tübingen and other partners, 1990s onwards","disc":["Stone tools about 125,000 years old (2011)","18 layers of occupation"],"refs":["Current World Archaeology (2025). Faya Palaeolandscape becomes only site in the Arab world to be awarded UNESCO World Heritage status in 2025.","UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Faya Palaeolandscape, inscribed 2025."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"mleiha","n":"Mleiha","na":"مليحة","c":"archaeological","s":"Capital of the kingdom of Uman · UNESCO tentative list","lat":25.1171,"lng":55.8716,"p":"Late pre-Islamic","dr":"c. 300 BC – 300 AD","el":"Al-Husn fort 55 × 50 m","ic":"caravan","era":"H","t0":2325,"t1":1725,"te":"A kingdom under the sand","ta":"مملكة تحت الرمال","se":"A trading capital with horse burials, a mint and amphorae from Rhodes","sa":"عاصمة تجارية بمدافن خيول ودار سكّ وجرار من رودس","desc":"A town east of Jebel Faya, about 50 km from Sharjah, occupied from roughly 300 BC to 300 AD as the centre of the kingdom of Uman, probably the Omana of classical sources. Remains include a fort (the al-Husn building, 55 × 50 m), a buttressed palace, tower tombs and houses with underground kitchens; finds include horse and hybrid-camel burials with a gold bridle, coins imitating Alexander's tetradrachm with a coin mould, the largest number of Rhodian amphorae in Arabia, and a 2016 bilingual Aramaic–Hasaitic tombstone naming the kingdom. A 1994 cemetery held 26 graves, 12 for camels. Abandoned after a violent attack in the 3rd century AD; submitted to the UNESCO Tentative List in 2023.","sig":"The eponymous site of the late pre-Islamic period in south-east Arabia and proof of an Arabian kingdom trading with the Hellenistic world.","ex":"Iraqi mission 1973; Belgian and French missions from the 1980s; Sharjah Archaeology Authority","disc":["Bilingual tombstone naming the kingdom of Uman (2016)","Horse burials with a gold bridle","Coin mould for Alexander-type tetradrachms"],"refs":["UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Mleiha, late pre-Islamic center of a south-east Arabian kingdom. Tentative List ref. 6643 (2023).","Emirates Natural History Group (2011). The United Arab Emirates throughout the ages."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"jebel-buhais","n":"Jebel Buhais Necropolis","na":"مقبرة جبل البحيص","c":"archaeological","s":"Neolithic herders’ cemetery","lat":25.017,"lng":55.7947,"p":"Neolithic to Iron Age","dr":"c. 5000 – 4000 BC (BHS 18)","el":"Limestone outcrop 340 m","ic":"shell","era":"P","t0":7025,"t1":6025,"te":"Winter camp of the herders","ta":"مخيم الرعاة الشتوي","se":"Nomadic herders buried their dead here for a thousand years","sa":"دفن الرعاة الرُّحّل موتاهم هنا ألف عام","desc":"A limestone outcrop 48 km south-east of Sharjah city, rising to about 340 m, on the ridge that runs from Madam to Mleiha. The Neolithic cemetery BHS 18, dated by radiocarbon to about 5000–4000 BC, is one of the key Neolithic sites of southern Arabia; its burials are read as those of nomadic herders who came inland for the winter, and the whole site has yielded the complete remains of about 600 individuals. Hafit-period beehive graves, a unique clover-shaped Wadi Suq tomb (BHS 66), Iron Age burials and an Iron Age fort lie on the same mountain. Excavated by Iraqi, French, Spanish and, from 1995, University of Tübingen teams (ten seasons at BHS 18, 1996–2005).","sig":"The largest Neolithic cemetery in the region, with the best evidence for the health, diet and movements of Arabia's first herders.","ex":"University of Tübingen (H.-P. Uerpmann) with the Sharjah Directorate of Antiquities, 1995–2005","disc":["About 600 individuals recovered","Unique clover-shaped tomb BHS 66"],"refs":["Uerpmann, H.-P., Uerpmann, M. & Jasim, S. A. (2006). Funeral Monuments and Human Remains from Jebel al-Buhais. Department of Culture and Information, Sharjah.","Jasim, S. A. (2012). The Necropolis of Jebel al-Buhais. Sharjah."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"saruq-al-hadid","n":"Saruq Al-Hadid","na":"ساروق الحديد","c":"archaeological","s":"Iron Age metal-working and ritual site","lat":24.65,"lng":55.35,"p":"Iron Age II","dr":"c. 1200 – 800 BC","el":"Dune field at the edge of the Empty Quarter","ic":"flame","era":"P","t0":3225,"t1":2825,"te":"Snakes of copper in the dunes","ta":"أفاعٍ من نحاس في الكثبان","se":"Smelting slag, arrowheads and copper snakes buried far from any ore","sa":"خبث صهر ورؤوس سهام وأفاعٍ نحاسية مدفونة بعيدًا عن أي خام","desc":"A site in the dunes of the Rub' al-Khali margin south of Dubai, excavated since 2002 by Jordanian, Australian, Spanish and Polish teams. Slag, ore nodules, crucibles and metallurgical tools show copper smelting and probably manufacture in Iron Age II, although no furnaces or moulds have yet been found; many copper arrowheads, axes and daggers were collected. The ritualised scattering of objects, with copper snakes, human figurines and soft-stone and alabaster vessels, suggests a ceremonial role. Location approximate.","sig":"A unique combination of industry and ritual in a place with no water or ore, still unexplained.","ex":"Dubai Municipality Architectural Heritage Department with Spanish (Sanisera), Australian (UNSW) and Polish (PCMA) teams, 2002 onwards","disc":["Copper snakes and figurines","Slag and crucibles without furnaces"],"refs":["Contreras Rodrigo, F. et al. (2017). Excavations in Area 2A at Sarūq al-Ḥadīd: Iron Age II evidence of copper production and ceremonial activities. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 47, 57–67."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"marawah-village","n":"Marawah Island Stone Age Village","na":"قرية مروح من العصر الحجري","c":"archaeological","s":"Oldest stone houses in the Gulf","lat":24.28,"lng":53.3,"p":"Neolithic","dr":"c. 8,000 – 7,500 years ago","el":"Island inside the Marawah Marine Biosphere Reserve","ic":"shell","era":"P","t0":8000,"t1":7000,"te":"The oldest pearl","ta":"أقدم لؤلؤة","se":"An 8,000-year-old village with stone houses, burials and a pearl less than a centimetre long","sa":"قرية عمرها 8,000 عام ببيوت حجرية ومدافن ولؤلؤة أقصر من سنتيمتر","desc":"Excavations by the Abu Dhabi Islands Archaeological Survey began in March 1998 on Marawah Island and revealed a Stone Age village: fireplaces of Late Stone Age hunters, then stone houses about 7,500 years old, the earliest permanent stone buildings found in the Arabian Gulf, with burials inside former homes, tools, jewellery and fishing gear. Two more settlements, MR I and MR II, lie nearby. In 2019 a natural pearl less than a centimetre long, dated to 5800–5600 BC, was found, the oldest known anywhere, pushing pearling back nearly 8,000 years.","sig":"The oldest architecture and the oldest pearl of the Gulf, on one small island.","ex":"Abu Dhabi Islands Archaeological Survey (ADIAS) 1998–2004; Department of Culture and Tourism – Abu Dhabi","disc":["Stone houses about 7,500 years old","Pearl dated 5800–5600 BC (2019)"],"refs":["The National (6 March 2026). Timeframe: when Abu Dhabi's 8,000-year-old village was first unearthed.","Beech, M. et al. Marawah excavation reports, Department of Culture and Tourism – Abu Dhabi."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"sir-bani-yas-monastery","n":"Sir Bani Yas Monastery","na":"دير صير بني ياس","c":"archaeological","s":"Church of the East monastery","lat":24.33,"lng":52.65,"p":"Late pre-Islamic to early Islamic","dr":"c. 600 – 750 AD","el":"East coast of Sir Bani Yas Island","ic":"platform","era":"H","t0":1425,"t1":1275,"te":"Monks on the island","ta":"رهبان على الجزيرة","se":"A 1,400-year-old church and monastery that traded with its Muslim neighbours","sa":"كنيسة ودير عمرهما 1,400 عام تاجرا مع جيرانهما المسلمين","desc":"A church and monastery on the eastern coast of Sir Bani Yas Island, about 200 km from Abu Dhabi, found in 1992 and at first not recognised as religious. About 1,400 years old, it has dormitories, a kitchen, cells and burials, with more buildings found in 2014 to the west, south-west and north. Finds include Christian crosses and a decorative frieze now in the Louvre Abu Dhabi, and evidence that the monks kept cattle and fished and traded with Muslim neighbours in the 7th and 8th centuries. Opened to the public in 2019; the island also holds the Arabian Wildlife Park.","sig":"The only known Christian monastery in the UAE and a rare window on the Gulf at the dawn of Islam.","ex":"Abu Dhabi Islands Archaeological Survey (G. King) 1992–1996; Department of Culture and Tourism – Abu Dhabi (P. Magee) 2014","disc":["Church with plaster crosses and frieze","Expansion found in 2014"],"refs":["Euronews (27 September 2019). Sir Bani Yas church and monastery: UAE's oldest Christian site opens to the public.","King, G. R. D. (1997). A Nestorian monastic settlement on the island of Sir Bani Yas, Abu Dhabi. Bulletin of SOAS 60(2)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"ed-dur","n":"Ed-Dur","na":"الدور","c":"archaeological","s":"Hellenistic-period city and temple","lat":25.59,"lng":55.63,"p":"Late pre-Islamic","dr":"1st century BC – 2nd century AD","el":"Umm Al Quwain coast","ic":"platform","era":"H","t0":2075,"t1":1825,"te":"A temple to the sun","ta":"معبد للشمس","se":"A coastal city whose temple named the sun god Shams in Aramaic","sa":"مدينة ساحلية سمّى معبدها إله الشمس «شمس» بالآرامية","desc":"One of the largest pre-Islamic sites in the Emirates, on the coast of Umm Al Quwain, partly excavated by an Iraqi mission in 1974 and by European missions from 1987. It belongs to the second phase of the Hellenistic period in the region; a Belgian team found a temple with three altars and an Aramaic inscription naming the solar deity Shams, together with imported glass and coins.","sig":"The main port of the Gulf coast in the early centuries AD, linking Mleiha inland to the Roman and Parthian world.","ex":"Iraqi mission 1974; Belgian, British, Danish and French missions 1987–1995","disc":["Temple of Shams with Aramaic inscription","Three altars"],"refs":["Emirates Natural History Group (2011). The United Arab Emirates throughout the ages.","Potts, D. T. (2003). Archaeology of the United Arab Emirates. Trident Press."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"umm-an-nar-island","n":"Umm an-Nar Island","na":"جزيرة أم النار","c":"archaeological","s":"Type site of the Umm an-Nar culture","lat":24.44,"lng":54.5,"p":"Umm an-Nar period","dr":"c. 2700 – 2000 BC","el":"50 above-ground tombs","ic":"mound","era":"P","t0":4725,"t1":4025,"te":"The island that named an age","ta":"الجزيرة التي سمّت عصرًا","se":"Fifty round tombs excavated by the Danes in 1959 gave a whole culture its name","sa":"خمسون قبرًا دائريًا نقّبها الدنماركيون عام 1959 أعطت ثقافة كاملة اسمها","desc":"A small island beside Abu Dhabi where a Danish expedition in 1959 excavated a cemetery of 50 above-ground circular tombs and a settlement of fishers and traders. The finds defined the Umm an-Nar period (2700–2000 BC), when the coast traded copper, pearls and pottery with Mesopotamia and the Indus. The island is now inside an industrial area and not open to visitors.","sig":"The reference site for the Bronze Age of the Emirates and Oman.","ex":"Danish Archaeological Expedition (P. V. Glob, G. Bibby), 1959–1965","disc":["50 circular tombs","Settlement of the 3rd millennium BC"],"refs":["Emirates Natural History Group (2011). The United Arab Emirates throughout the ages.","Frifelt, K. (1991). The Island of Umm an-Nar. Jutland Archaeological Society."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"julfar-kush","n":"Julfar (Kush and Shimal)","na":"جلفار (كوش والشمل)","c":"archaeological","s":"Medieval port of Ras Al Khaimah","lat":25.85,"lng":56.0,"p":"Islamic","dr":"13th – 17th century AD","el":"Shimal plain, Ras Al Khaimah","ic":"jar","era":"H","t0":775,"t1":375,"te":"The port of Chinese porcelain","ta":"ميناء الخزف الصيني","se":"A trading town that shipped pearls and horses and imported porcelain from China","sa":"بلدة تجارية شحنت اللؤلؤ والخيول واستوردت الخزف من الصين","desc":"The medieval port of Julfar, prominent in trade by the early 14th century and described by Duarte Barbosa in 1517, is now placed at Kush in the Shimal plain north of Ras Al Khaimah city; its oldest remains date to the 13th century. Excavations uncovered Chinese and East Asian porcelain among the houses of a town that traded pearls and horses across the Indian Ocean. Location approximate.","sig":"The Emirates' main link to the Indian Ocean trade of the Middle Ages, and the home port of the navigator Ahmad ibn Majid.","ex":"British (Kennet), Japanese, French and German missions, 1970s–1990s","disc":["Chinese porcelain in a 14th-century port","Town remains from the 13th century"],"refs":["Emirates Natural History Group (2011). The United Arab Emirates throughout the ages.","Kennet, D. (2004). Sasanian and Islamic Pottery from Ras al-Khaimah. Archaeopress."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"jebel-hafeet","n":"Jebel Hafeet","na":"جبل حفيت","c":"geological","s":"Folded Eocene mountain","lat":24.0586,"lng":55.7775,"p":"Eocene to Miocene","dr":"Rocks 56 – 16 million years old; folded 28 – 15 million years ago","el":"1,249 m · 26 km long","ic":"cliff","era":"D","t0":56000000.0,"t1":15000000.0,"te":"The mountain that leans","ta":"الجبل المائل","se":"A 26 km fold with one side tipped over, hot springs at its foot","sa":"طيّة بطول 26 كم انقلب أحد جانبيها، وينابيع حارة عند قدمها","desc":"An isolated mountain of 1,249 m on the UAE–Oman border near Al Ain, 26 km long and 4–5 km wide, trending NNW–SSE. It is an asymmetric anticline of shallow-marine limestone, marl and evaporite with corals, foraminifera and bryozoa, ranging from early Eocene in the core to early Miocene on the flanks; the west limb dips about 30° and the east limb is overturned at about 70°, probably over a backthrust, with folding dated to the late Oligocene to middle Miocene. Hot springs of about 40 °C at Green Mubazzarah come from water circulating 2 km down through faults beneath the fold. Caves reach at least 150 m. About 500 Hafit-period tombs lie on its foothills.","sig":"The UAE's best-exposed anticline, the source of Al Ain's hot springs and the backdrop to its oldest tombs.","ex":"Geological surveys of the UAE (Ministry of Energy / British Geological Survey)","disc":["East limb overturned at 70°","Hot springs at 40 °C"],"refs":["Wikipedia contributors (2026). Jebel Hafeet, citing geological surveys of the Al Ain area.","Searle, M. P. & Ali, M. Y. (2009). Structural and tectonic evolution of the Jabal Sumeini–Al Ain–Buraimi region. GeoArabia 14(1)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"buhais-geology-park","n":"Buhais Geology Park","na":"حديقة البحيص الجيولوجية","c":"geological","s":"Cretaceous sea floor on show","lat":25.03,"lng":55.78,"p":"Cretaceous","dr":"Rocks at least 93 million years old","el":"Opened 21 January 2020","ic":"fossil","era":"D","t0":93000000.0,"t1":66000000.0,"te":"Fossils from before the mountains","ta":"أحافير من قبل الجبال","se":"Ammonites and sea urchins from a sea that covered Sharjah 93 million years ago","sa":"أمونيتات وقنافذ بحر من بحر غطّى الشارقة قبل 93 مليون عام","desc":"A visitor park opened on 21 January 2020 on the limestone ridge of Jebel Buhais, displaying fossils and features dating back at least 93 million years to the Cretaceous, when the area lay beneath a shallow sea before the Hajar Mountains rose. The ridge runs almost continuously from Madam to Mleiha and also carries the Neolithic to Iron Age cemeteries of Jebel Buhais.","sig":"The easiest place in the UAE to see the marine rocks that predate the ophiolite and the mountains.","ex":"Sharjah Environment and Protected Areas Authority","disc":["Cretaceous fossils at least 93 million years old"],"refs":["Sharjah Environment and Protected Areas Authority. Buhais Geology Park.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Jebel Buhais."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"moreeb-dune","n":"Moreeb Dune (Liwa)","na":"تل مرعب (ليوا)","c":"geological","s":"Tallest dune in the UAE","lat":22.99,"lng":53.78,"p":"Quaternary","dr":"Active today","el":"About 300 m high · slip face 50°","ic":"dune","era":"D","t0":11700,"t1":0,"te":"A wall of sand","ta":"جدار من رمل","se":"A 300 m dune at the edge of the Empty Quarter, 1.6 km long","sa":"كثيب بارتفاع 300 م على حافة الربع الخالي، طوله 1.6 كم","desc":"A dune about 300 m high and 1,600 m long with a slip face reported at 50°, about 25 km south of Liwa Oasis and 250 km from Abu Dhabi, at the northern edge of the Rub' al Khali (Empty Quarter). The tallest dune in the UAE, it hosts the Liwa Moreeb Dune Festival of hill-climb racing. The dunes of Liwa are the end of a sand sea that stretches across southern Arabia.","sig":"The UAE's doorway to the largest sand desert on Earth.","ex":"Abu Dhabi Department of Culture and Tourism (Liwa festival)","disc":["About 300 m high","Slip face about 50°"],"refs":["Wikipedia contributors (2026). Moreeb Dune, citing Liwa Sports Club and Visit Abu Dhabi."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"wadi-wurayah","n":"Wadi Wurayah National Park","na":"حديقة وادي الوريعة الوطنية","c":"ecology","s":"Mountain park on ancient ocean crust · World Heritage 2026","lat":25.4,"lng":56.25,"p":"Living today","dr":"National park 2009 · World Heritage 2026","el":"225 km² · 300 springs · 60 streams","ic":"river","era":"N","t0":17,"t1":0,"te":"Water in the mountains of the sea floor","ta":"ماء في جبال قاع البحر","se":"Springs and tahr on rocks that were once ocean crust, 90 million years old","sa":"ينابيع ووعول على صخور كانت يومًا قشرة محيط عمرها 90 مليون عام","desc":"A 225 km² park in the Hajar Mountains of Fujairah with more than 300 springs and 60 streams, designated the UAE's first mountain protected area in 2009 and inscribed on the World Heritage List at the 2026 session in Busan as the country's first natural site. Its rocks are ophiolite, fragments of oceanic crust about 90 million years old considered among the best preserved in the world. Surveys since 2006 have recorded 1,099 species, including the endangered Arabian tahr (one of its three remaining habitats), the Arabian caracal rediscovered in 2025, Blanford's fox and a dragonfly once thought extinct.","sig":"The UAE's richest mountain ecosystem and its first natural World Heritage site, on a slice of the ocean floor.","ex":"Fujairah Environment Authority with Emirates Nature–WWF, 2006 onwards","disc":["1,099 species recorded","Arabian caracal rediscovered 2025","Ophiolite about 90 million years old"],"refs":["Scene Traveller (26 July 2026). Wadi Wurayah becomes UAE's first UNESCO natural World Heritage site.","Ramsar Sites Information Service. Wadi Wurayah National Park (site 1932), designated 2010."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-wathba-wetland","n":"Al Wathba Wetland Reserve","na":"محمية الوثبة للأراضي الرطبة","c":"ecology","s":"Flamingo breeding wetland · Ramsar","lat":24.26,"lng":54.75,"p":"Living today","dr":"Reserve 1998 · Ramsar 2013","el":"5 km²","ic":"bird","era":"N","t0":28,"t1":0,"te":"Where flamingos chose to nest","ta":"حيث اختار الفلامنغو أن يعشّش","se":"Up to 3,600 flamingos on lakes fed by treated city water","sa":"حتى 3,600 طائر فلامنغو على بحيرات تغذّيها مياه المدينة المعالجة","desc":"A 5 km² wetland of lakes and sabkha south-east of Abu Dhabi city, declared a reserve in 1998 and the emirate's first Ramsar site in 2013, with water levels partly maintained by tertiary-treated wastewater. Greater flamingos first bred successfully in December 1998, fledging 10 young in April 1999, and have bred every year since 2011; January counts in 2013–2018 ranged from 2,500 to 3,600 birds. Recorded diversity: 236 bird, 39 plant, 15 reptile, 10 mammal and 354 invertebrate species, about 21% of the emirate's terrestrial species. Visitors rose from 2,560 in 2014–15 to 5,780 in 2017–18.","sig":"The first regular flamingo breeding site in the Gulf states and a model of a wetland made with recycled water.","ex":"Environment Agency – Abu Dhabi","disc":["First flamingo breeding, December 1998","354 invertebrate species"],"refs":["Campbell, O., Javed, S., Al Dhaheri, S. et al. (2018). Al Wathba Wetland Reserve, Abu Dhabi emirate: successful mixing of birds and people. Sandgrouse Supplement 4.","Ramsar Sites Information Service. Al Wathba Wetland Reserve (site 2142)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"ras-al-khor","n":"Ras Al Khor Wildlife Sanctuary","na":"محمية رأس الخور","c":"ecology","s":"Flamingos at the end of Dubai Creek · Ramsar","lat":25.1917,"lng":55.3219,"p":"Living today","dr":"Ramsar site since 29 August 2007","el":"620 ha","ic":"bird","era":"N","t0":19,"t1":0,"te":"Flamingos among the towers","ta":"فلامنغو بين الأبراج","se":"Twenty thousand waterbirds winter in a lagoon ringed by the city","sa":"عشرون ألف طائر مائي تشتو في بحيرة تحيط بها المدينة","desc":"A 620 ha sanctuary of mangrove, lagoon, intertidal mud and sand flats and saline flats at the end of the 14 km Dubai Creek, designated a Ramsar site on 29 August 2007. More than 20,000 waterbirds of 67 species winter here, including more than 3,000 greater flamingos; more than 1% of the regional population of broad-billed sandpipers passes through, and the threatened sociable lapwing and greater spotted eagle occur. Over 450 animal and 47 plant species are recorded, and about 10,000 people visit each year.","sig":"A wetland of international importance surviving inside one of the fastest-growing cities on Earth.","ex":"Dubai Municipality (Environment Department)","disc":["More than 20,000 wintering waterbirds","More than 3,000 greater flamingos"],"refs":["Ramsar Sites Information Service. Ras Al Khor Wildlife Sanctuary (site 1715), designated 2007."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"khor-kalba","n":"Khor Kalba Mangroves (Al Qurm)","na":"قرم خور كلباء","c":"ecology","s":"Oldest mangrove woodland in the region · Ramsar","lat":24.9981,"lng":56.3625,"p":"Living today","dr":"Ramsar site since 10 March 2013","el":"1,494 ha","ic":"mangrove","era":"N","t0":30,"t1":0,"te":"The kingfisher’s only home","ta":"بيت الرفراف الوحيد","se":"Tall old mangroves where a kingfisher found nowhere else in the world breeds","sa":"قرم عالٍ عتيق يتكاثر فيه رفراف لا يوجد في أي مكان آخر في العالم","desc":"A 1,494 ha protected area on the Gulf of Oman coast of Sharjah near the Oman border, listed under Ramsar on 10 March 2013. Its Avicennia marina trees are the tallest and the most extensive mature mangrove woodland in the biogeographic region. The Kalba white-collared kingfisher (Todiramphus chloris kalbaensis), an endemic subspecies, breeds almost exclusively here; Sykes's warbler has one of its two regional breeding sites; hawksbill and green turtles enter the creeks at high tide to feed. Habitats also include beach, tidal channels, salt marsh and Acacia woodland.","sig":"A relict mangrove forest that holds a bird found nowhere else on Earth.","ex":"Sharjah Environment and Protected Areas Authority","disc":["Endemic Kalba kingfisher subspecies","Tallest mangroves in the region"],"refs":["Ramsar Sites Information Service. Mangrove and Al Hafeya Protected Area in Khor Kalba (site 2125), designated 2013."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"bu-tinah-dugongs","n":"Marawah Marine Biosphere Reserve and Bu Tinah","na":"محمية مروح البحرية وبو طينة","c":"ecology","s":"Densest dugong population in the world","lat":24.6,"lng":53.08,"p":"Living today","dr":"UNESCO biosphere reserve 2007","el":"Shallow seagrass banks west of Abu Dhabi","ic":"dolphin","era":"N","t0":27,"t1":0,"te":"Three thousand sea cows","ta":"ثلاثة آلاف بقرة بحر","se":"Abu Dhabi’s seagrass holds the second-largest dugong population after Australia","sa":"تحتضن أعشاب أبوظبي البحرية ثاني أكبر تجمّع للأطوم بعد أستراليا","desc":"The UAE's first UNESCO biosphere reserve, recognised in 2007, covering seagrass beds, coral reefs and mangroves around Marawah Island west of Abu Dhabi, with four turtle species and 70 fish species. The Environment Agency – Abu Dhabi protects about 3,000 dugongs, mostly in Marawah and the Al Yasat protected area, the largest population after Australia; Bu Tinah, inside the reserve, is described as the densest dugong population in the world. Dugongs have been studied here since 1999 by aerial survey, satellite tagging and genetics; threats are abandoned nets, habitat loss, pollution and boat strikes.","sig":"The most important dugong habitat outside Australia.","ex":"Environment Agency – Abu Dhabi, 1999 onwards","disc":["About 3,000 dugongs in Abu Dhabi waters","Bu Tinah: densest dugong population known"],"refs":["Environment Agency – Abu Dhabi. Dugong (Dugong dugon) species page.","Gulf News (2 November 2007). Marawah is UAE's first biosphere reserve."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"tell-abraq","n":"Tell Abraq","na":"تل أبرق","c":"archaeological","s":"Bronze–Iron Age mound and Umm an-Nar fortress tower · excavated since 1989","lat":25.4878,"lng":55.5519,"p":"Umm an-Nar, Wadi Suq and Iron Age","dr":"c. 2200 BC – AD 200","el":"Tower 40 m across, 8 m high","ic":"mound","era":"P","t0":4225,"t1":1825,"te":"The great tower of Magan","ta":"البرج العظيم لأرض مجان","se":"A four-hectare mound on the Sharjah–Umm Al Quwain line with the largest Umm an-Nar fortress tower in the UAE and a tomb of about 410 people","sa":"تلّ بمساحة أربعة هكتارات على خط الشارقة وأم القيوين يضم أكبر برج حصن من حضارة أم النار في الإمارات ومدفناً لنحو 410 أشخاص","desc":"Tell Abraq is a settlement mound of about four hectares rising roughly 10 m above the sabkha, described as the best preserved and largest prehistoric settlement in the lower Arabian Gulf. A circular mud-brick fortress faced with stone, about 40 m in diameter and 8 m high, was built around 2200 BC (charcoal dated 2461–2199 BC), with a stone-lined well at its centre. An Umm an-Nar tomb about 6 m across, 10 m west of the tower, held the commingled remains of about 410 individuals, including a young woman with polio. Occupation ran from c. 2200 BC to AD 200, and finds include Harappan weights, Barbar pottery, Bactrian ivory and more than 100,000 animal bones, the largest faunal assemblage in the Arabian Peninsula.","sig":"The site's long sequence defined the Iron Age I–III chronology of the UAE and shows the region's trade with Mesopotamia, Iran, Bahrain and the Indus.","ex":"University of Copenhagen (D.T. Potts, 1989–1998); Bryn Mawr College and University of Tübingen (P. Magee, from 2007); Italian Archaeological Mission in Umm al-Quwain (M. Degli Esposti, from 2019)","disc":["Tomb held about 410 individuals, 276 of them adults","Largest Umm an-Nar fortress tower excavated in the UAE"],"refs":["Potts, D.T. (1993). Four Seasons of Excavation at Tell Abraq (1989–1993). Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 23.","Magee, P. (2017). Tell Abraq during the second and first millennia BC. Arabian Archaeology and Epigraphy 28(2).","Degli Esposti, M. et al. (2022). Renewed excavations at Tell Abraq, Umm al-Quwain, 2019–2020. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 51."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"shimal-tombs","n":"Shimal","na":"شمل","c":"archaeological","s":"Largest pre-Islamic site in Ras Al Khaimah · c. 250 tombs and a hilltop fort","lat":25.8181,"lng":56.0317,"p":"Umm an-Nar to Wadi Suq, with a medieval fort","dr":"c. 2600 BC – 18th century AD","el":"Tomb Sh 222: 11.3 m across, about 300 burials","ic":"mound","era":"P","t0":4625,"t1":250,"te":"A city of the dead below the mountains","ta":"مدينة الموتى تحت الجبال","se":"About 250 monumental tombs at the foot of the mountains north of Ras Al Khaimah, settled for at least 4,500 years, with a medieval fort on the outcrop above","sa":"نحو 250 مدفناً ضخماً عند سفح الجبال شمال رأس الخيمة، استُوطن المكان منذ 4,500 سنة على الأقل، ويعلوه حصن من العصور الوسطى","desc":"Shimal, about 8 km north-east of Ras Al Khaimah town, is the largest pre-Islamic site in the emirate and has been settled for at least 4,500 years. Its cemetery holds about 250 tombs of several types, many monumental: the Umm an-Nar tomb Sh 222 is 11.3 m across and held about 300 burials dated to c. 2200–2000 BC, while Wadi Suq tombs such as Sh 99 (16 m long, at least 66 individuals) and Sh 102 (about 140 individuals, 15th–13th centuries BC) date to the 2nd millennium BC. Finds include soft-stone vessels, bronze and copper weapons, beads and pottery possibly imported from Bahrain and south-eastern Iran. On the outcrop above the village stands the so-called 'Palace of the Queen of Sheba', a fortification probably no older than Julfar (14th–18th centuries AD).","sig":"Shimal is the type site for the Wadi Suq period in the northern Emirates and shows continuous settlement from the Bronze Age to the Islamic era.","ex":"British and German archaeologists since 1976, chiefly a team from the University of Göttingen in the mid-1980s; finds in the National Museum of Ras Al Khaimah","disc":["Cemetery of about 250 tombs, some holding over 300 burials","Harappan-linked beads and pottery show Indus contacts"],"refs":["Emirates Natural History Group. Shimal Archaeological Site (enhg.org).","Hellyer, P. and Potts, D. (2012). Fifty Years of Emirates Archaeology. Abu Dhabi, pp. 216–221.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Shimal, citing Potts (1988) Araby the Blest."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-sinniyah-island","n":"Al Sinniyah Island","na":"جزيرة السينية","c":"archaeological","s":"Late antique monastery and the oldest known pearling town in the Arabian Gulf","lat":25.58,"lng":55.6,"p":"Late antique to early Islamic","dr":"Late 6th – mid 8th century AD","el":"Town of about 10–12 hectares","ic":"shell","era":"H","t0":1450,"t1":1275,"te":"Pearls before the dawn of Islam","ta":"لآلئ ما قبل فجر الإسلام","se":"A monastery found in 2022 and a town of hundreds of beach-rock houses, pearls and the oldest well-dated diver's weight in the Emirates","sa":"دير اكتُشف عام 2022 وبلدة من مئات البيوت المبنية بحجر الشاطئ، مع لآلئ وأقدم ثقل غواص مؤرخ في الإمارات","desc":"On Al Sinniyah Island in the Umm Al Quwain lagoon, archaeologists announced in March 2023 the oldest pearling town yet found in the Arabian Gulf, covering about 12 hectares with hundreds of houses and several thousand residents. The town was occupied for about 200 years, from the late 6th or early 7th century to the mid-8th century AD, and sits beside a Christian monastery unearthed in 2022 and dated to the 7th–8th centuries. Excavations found pearls, a pearl diver's weight described as the earliest well-dated example in the Emirates, a 6th–7th century Sasanian stamp seal, oyster-shell middens of possibly millions of shells and ceramics of which about 12% were imported from India. Large courtyard houses of merchants stood among small one- or two-room houses of fishermen, all built of local beach rock.","sig":"The island shows that pearling towns and Christian communities flourished in the Gulf a generation before Islam and continued for two generations after it.","ex":"Umm Al Quwain Department of Tourism and Archaeology, UAE University (Prof Timothy Power), Italian Archaeological Mission in Umm Al Quwain, Institute for the Study of the Ancient World at New York University","disc":["Monastery unearthed in 2022; pearling town announced March 2023","About 12% of the pottery was imported from India"],"refs":["Power, T., Degli Esposti, M., Hoyland, R. et al. (2024). Excavations at a late antique to early Islamic pearling town and monastery on Sīnīya Island, Umm al-Quwain. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 53: 211–229.","Dennehy, J. (2023). Oldest pearling town in the Arabian Gulf discovered in Umm Al Quwain. The National, 20 March 2023."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"jumeirah-site","n":"Jumeirah Archaeological Site","na":"موقع جميرا الأثري","c":"archaeological","s":"Abbasid caravan town inside modern Dubai · managed by Dubai Culture","lat":25.1968,"lng":55.2418,"p":"Islamic, Abbasid period","dr":"About the 10th century AD","el":"Fenced plot of 80,000 m²","ic":"caravan","era":"H","t0":1100,"t1":900,"te":"A caravan city under the villas","ta":"مدينة قوافل تحت الفلل","se":"An 80,000 m² Abbasid trading settlement with a mosque, caravanserai, souq and ruler's house, first excavated in 1969","sa":"مستوطنة تجارية عباسية بمساحة 80,000 م² تضم مسجداً وخاناً وسوقاً وبيت الحاكم، بدأ تنقيبها عام 1969","desc":"The Jumeirah Archaeological Site is a fenced plot of about 80,000 m² (8 ha) in Jumeirah 2, Dubai, preserving the remains of a trading settlement of the Abbasid period, around the 10th century AD. It was first explored in 1969 by Dimitri Baramki of the American University of Beirut, continued by an Iraqi team in 1974, and has been excavated by Dubai's own department since 1993. Seven structures have been identified, including a mosque, a caravanserai, a souq, a workshop and a large residential building believed to be the ruler's house, along a caravan route linking India and China with Oman and Iraq. Finds include Abbasid coins, glazed pottery from southern Iraq, Chinese stoneware and stucco decoration; the site is owned and managed by the Dubai Culture and Arts Authority.","sig":"It is the main physical evidence that an organised Islamic trading town existed in the Dubai area a thousand years ago.","ex":"Dimitri Baramki, American University of Beirut (1969); Iraqi mission (1974); Dubai Department of Tourism and Commerce Marketing / Dubai Culture (since 1993); Polish–Emirati study of the stucco (Juchniewicz and Lic)","disc":["Nine structures of the old town survived modern development","Chinese ceramics and Iraqi pottery prove long-distance trade"],"refs":["Juchniewicz, K. and Lic, A. (2023). Abbasid Jumeirah, Dubai. An Overview of the Site and Its Architectural Stucco Decoration. Études et Travaux 36: 57–81.","Juchniewicz, K. et al. (2025). Breeze of Continuity: New Evidence for the Occupation of Jumeirah Throughout the Islamic Period. Arabian Archaeology and Epigraphy 36(1).","Gulf News. Jumeirah: Digging up the past."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"dhayah-fort","n":"Dhayah Fort","na":"قلعة ضاية","c":"archaeological","s":"Highest hilltop fort in the UAE · Bronze Age hill, 1819 siege","lat":25.8844,"lng":56.0605,"p":"Late Bronze Age settlement; 18th–19th century fort","dr":"c. 1600 BC – AD 1819","el":"Hill about 70 m high","ic":"wall","era":"H","t0":3625,"t1":205,"te":"Three days without water","ta":"ثلاثة أيام بلا ماء","se":"A mud-brick fort on a 70 m hill above Rams where about 400 people held out for three days in December 1819 before the General Maritime Treaty","sa":"قلعة من الطوب الطيني على تل بارتفاع 70 متراً فوق رمس صمد فيها نحو 400 شخص ثلاثة أيام في ديسمبر 1819 قبل المعاهدة البحرية العامة","desc":"Dhayah Fort stands on a twin-peaked hill about 70 m high between the date gardens and the Jebel Jais mountains in Ras Al Khaimah and is described as the highest hilltop fort in the UAE. The hill was used for settlement and fortification in the Late Bronze Age (c. 1600–1300 BC), and twelve Wadi Suq tombs of c. 2000–1300 BC lie at its base, four of them excavated. In December 1819 a British landing force marched inland and bombarded the fort with mortars and two 24-pounder cannons dragged four miles from the coast; about 400 people held out for three days without water before surrendering at 10:30 on 22 December, after which the fort was demolished. The present fort was rebuilt after 1819 and restored in the late 1990s, and the fall of Dhayah paved the way for the General Maritime Treaty of 1820.","sig":"Dhayah links a Bronze Age hill settlement with the 1819 events that reshaped the political history of the Gulf coast.","ex":"Ras Al Khaimah Department of Antiquities and Museums; restored in the late 1990s","disc":["Twelve Wadi Suq tombs at the base, four excavated","Of 400 who surrendered in 1819, only 177 were men of fighting age"],"refs":["Ras Al Khaimah Department of Antiquities and Museums. Dhayah Fort (rakheritage.rak.ae).","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Dhayah Fort, citing Lorimer (1915) Gazetteer and United Service Magazine.","McNabb, A. (2025). Children of the Seven Sands. Motivate Media Group."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"bidaa-bint-saud","n":"Bidaa Bint Saud","na":"بدع بنت سعود","c":"archaeological","s":"Hafit tombs and Iron Age falaj · World Heritage (Al Ain, 2011)","lat":24.3853,"lng":55.7183,"p":"Hafit to Iron Age","dr":"c. 3200 – 300 BC","el":"Iron Age hall 10 × 13 m with 12 columns","ic":"well","era":"P","t0":5225,"t1":2325,"te":"Where the falaj was born","ta":"حيث وُلد الفلج","se":"A rocky outcrop north of Al Ain with 5,000-year-old tombs and one of the earliest falaj irrigation systems known, with a mud-brick water house","sa":"نتوء صخري شمال العين يضم مدافن عمرها 5,000 سنة وواحداً من أقدم أنظمة الأفلاج المعروفة مع بيت طيني لتوزيع الماء","desc":"Bidaa Bint Saud lies about 15–25 km north of Al Ain, where the outcrop of Garn bin Saud rises about 40 m above a probable caravan route. Collapsed domed tombs of the Hafit period (3200–2600 BC) line the eastern face, alongside Wadi Suq burials and Iron Age collective graves that yielded pottery, stone vessels, dagger blades, bronze arrowheads and beads. Two Iron Age falaj channels have been partly excavated, with sandstone-lined shafts, a stepped underground access and a large open cistern; pottery dating places the falaj before the Achaemenid Empire, supporting a south-east Arabian origin for the system. Beside the falaj stands a mud-brick building with a 10 × 13 m hall, walls up to 160 cm high and 12 columns, probably a water distribution point. The site is part of the Cultural Sites of Al Ain inscribed on the World Heritage List in 2011.","sig":"Its Iron Age falaj is key evidence that underground irrigation was invented in south-east Arabia rather than imported from Persia.","ex":"Al Ain Department of Antiquities (Walid Yasin Al Tikriti); Department of Culture and Tourism – Abu Dhabi; finds in Al Ain National Museum","disc":["Falaj dated earlier than the Achaemenid Empire","World Heritage 2011 as part of the Al Ain sites (ref. 1343)"],"refs":["Al Tikriti, W.Y. (2002). The south-east Arabian origin of the falaj system. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 32: 117–138.","Department of Culture and Tourism – Abu Dhabi. Bidaa Bint Saud (abudhabiculture.ae).","Potts, D.T., Al Naboodah, H. and Hellyer, P. (2003). Archaeology of the United Arab Emirates, pp. 174–177."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"jebel-jais","n":"Jebel Jais","na":"جبل جيس","c":"geological","s":"Highest peak in the UAE, 1,934 m · Hajar Mountains","lat":25.94,"lng":56.13,"p":"Hajar Mountains, late Cretaceous collision","dr":"More than 70 million years","el":"Summit 1,934 m above sea level","ic":"cliff","era":"D","t0":70000000,"t1":0,"te":"The roof of the Emirates","ta":"سقف الإمارات","se":"A 1,934 m limestone summit in the Hajar range raised when the Arabian and Eurasian plates collided in the late Cretaceous","sa":"قمة جيرية بارتفاع 1,934 متراً في سلسلة الحجر ارتفعت عندما اصطدمت الصفيحتان العربية والأوراسية في أواخر العصر الطباشيري","desc":"Jebel Jais in Ras Al Khaimah rises to 1,934 m above sea level and is the highest peak in the United Arab Emirates. It belongs to the Al Hajar range, about 700 km long and 100 km wide across the UAE and Oman, whose jagged peaks were shaped by continental collision along the Arabian–Eurasian plate boundary in the late Cretaceous. The mountain contains marine and terrestrial sedimentary rocks with fossils, and the range holds ophiolites, among the few places where oceanic rocks can be studied on land; its formations are more than 70 million years old. The summit road leads to the UAE's highest viewing deck park, and the Jais Flight zipline is 2.83 km long.","sig":"It is the highest point of the Emirates and the clearest window on the uplift of the Hajar Mountains.","ex":"Ras Al Khaimah Tourism Development Authority manages the visitor area","disc":["Hajar range about 700 km long and 100 km wide","Rocks more than 70 million years old"],"refs":["Visit Ras Al Khaimah. The ancient story beneath the slopes: Jebel Jais history and geology explained.","Visit Jebel Jais (RAKTDA). Jebel Jais official page."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"fossil-rock","n":"Fossil Rock (Jebel Maleihah)","na":"جبل الفوسيل (جبل مليحة)","c":"geological","s":"Marine limestone ridge full of Cretaceous fossils · Mleiha National Park","lat":25.13,"lng":55.86,"p":"Late Cretaceous – Paleocene marine limestone over the Samail Ophiolite","dr":"More than 80 million years","el":"Shells, snails, clams and sea urchins in the rock","ic":"fossil","era":"D","t0":85000000,"t1":60000000,"te":"A sea floor lifted into the desert","ta":"قاع بحر رُفع إلى الصحراء","se":"A limestone ridge north of Jebel Faya packed with snail, clam and sea-urchin fossils from a shallow sea more than 80 million years ago","sa":"حافة جيرية شمال جبل الفاية تمتلئ بأحافير القواقع والمحار وقنافذ البحر من بحر ضحل قبل أكثر من 80 مليون سنة","desc":"Fossil Rock lies a few kilometres north of Jebel Faya in Sharjah, beside the limestone ridge of Jebel Maleihah at the northern edge of the Rub' al Khali. The ridges are marine limestone deposited over tens of millions of years from the Late Cretaceous to the early–mid Paleocene on top of the Samail Ophiolite, one of the world's largest exposures of ancient oceanic crust thrust onto Arabia. Coral fragments and marine invertebrate fossils are visible throughout, and Fossil Rock itself holds an abundance of snail, clam and sea-urchin remains more than 80 million years old. Nearby Jebel Faya stands 412 m above sea level, and the surrounding Faya Palaeolandscape was inscribed on the World Heritage List in 2025.","sig":"It is the most accessible place in the UAE to see a Cretaceous sea floor and the ophiolite beneath it.","ex":"Mleiha National Park, Sharjah; NASA Earth Observatory imagery","disc":["Limestone deposited on top of the Samail Ophiolite","Fossils of fish, ammonites and shells over 80 million years old"],"refs":["NASA Earth Observatory (2025). Digging Back in Time in the UAE (Landsat 8 image of 23 October 2025).","SceneTraveller. The Desert is a Ceiling to a Forgotten Sea at This Sharjah Park."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"mleisa-elephant-trackway","n":"Mleisa Elephant Trackway","na":"مسار أقدام الفيلة في مليسا","c":"geological","s":"Seven-million-year-old elephant footprints · Baynunah Formation, Al Dhafra","lat":23.68,"lng":53.95,"p":"Baynunah Formation, Late Miocene","dr":"About 6–8 million years ago","el":"Single trackway 260 m long over 5 hectares","ic":"fossil","era":"D","t0":8000000,"t1":6000000,"te":"A herd walks across the mud","ta":"قطيع يعبر الطين","se":"Hundreds of fossil footprints of a herd of four-tusked elephant ancestors, with a lone male's 260 m trail, on a 5-hectare plain in western Abu Dhabi","sa":"مئات من آثار أقدام متحجرة لقطيع من أسلاف الفيلة ذات الأنياب الأربعة، مع مسار ذكر منفرد بطول 260 متراً، على سهل مساحته 5 هكتارات غرب أبوظبي","desc":"Mleisa 1, in the Al Dhafra region of western Abu Dhabi, preserves hundreds of elephant footprints in the Baynunah Formation, dated to about seven million years ago in the Late Miocene. A solitary male's trackway runs 260 m, while other tracks were left by herding females and juveniles; the site covers about 5 hectares and is one of the largest trackway sites in the world. The 2012 study in Biology Letters by an international team including Faysal Bibi, Brian Kraatz and Andrew Hill used stride lengths to show a herd of adults and a young calf. The Baynunah Formation, laid down by a braided river system with channels three metres deep, has also yielded four-tusked elephants, a primitive hippopotamus, giraffes, six antelope species, crocodiles, turtles, monkeys and ostriches; five further elephant trackway sites have since been found in Al Dhafra.","sig":"It is the oldest direct evidence of herd behaviour in elephant ancestors and shows a wooded, river-crossed Arabia seven million years ago.","ex":"Department of Culture and Tourism – Abu Dhabi; international team (Germany, France, USA, UAE) led by Faysal Bibi","disc":["Published in Biology Letters, 22 February 2012","Coastline then lay about 300 km east of today"],"refs":["Bibi, F., Kraatz, B., Hill, A. et al. (2012). Early evidence for complex social structure in Proboscidea from a late Miocene trackway site in the United Arab Emirates. Biology Letters (Royal Society).","Department of Culture and Tourism – Abu Dhabi. Mleisa Elephant Trackway; Baynunah Geological Formation Fossil Sites (abudhabiculture.ae)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"abu-dhabi-coastal-sabkha","n":"Abu Dhabi coastal sabkha (Al Dabbiya)","na":"سبخة أبوظبي الساحلية (الدبّية)","c":"geological","s":"World type locality of the sabkha · Holocene carbonate–evaporite flats","lat":24.28,"lng":54.18,"p":"Holocene coastal carbonates and evaporites","dr":"Last 4,000–5,000 years","el":"Salt flats up to 15 km wide","ic":"salt","era":"N","t0":5000,"t1":0,"te":"The salt flats that wrote the textbooks","ta":"سبخات دخلت كتب الجيولوجيا","se":"The coastal salt flats west of Abu Dhabi, the world's reference sabkha since Graham Evans found anhydrite forming just below their surface in the 1960s","sa":"السبخات الساحلية غرب أبوظبي، المرجع العالمي للسبخات منذ أن وجد غراهام إيفانز الأنهيدريت يتكوّن تحت سطحها في الستينيات","desc":"The coast of Abu Dhabi is the accepted type locality for sabkhas: a shallow carbonate ramp with a tidal range of 1–2 m where salt flats cover about 36,000 km² and extend up to 15 km inland from dune fields. Groundwater lies never deeper than 1.5 m and is concentrated to about ten times seawater, precipitating calcite, dolomite and gypsum with lesser anhydrite, halite and carnallite; net evaporation has averaged 6 cm a year over the last 4,000–5,000 years and the flats prograde about 1 m per 1,000 years. Prof Graham Evans of Imperial College London first documented the flats in 1962 and found anhydrite forming just below the surface at Saadiyat and Musaffah, a discovery now in geology textbooks. His associate Dr Tony Kirkham identified a 30 km stretch of coast at Al Dabbiya as of exceptional significance and campaigned for protection from infilling and industry.","sig":"These flats are the global reference for how evaporite minerals form in arid coasts and a gauge of past sea-level change.","ex":"Graham Evans and colleagues (Imperial College London, since 1962); Tony Kirkham; Alsharhan and Kendall (satellite and field surveys)","disc":["Anhydrite found forming at the surface, overturning deep-water theories","Abu Dhabi salt flats cover about 36,000 km²"],"refs":["Evans, G. et al. (1969). Stratigraphy and geologic history of the sabkha, Abu Dhabi. Sedimentology 12.","Todorova, V. (2010). UK scientist leads fight to save capital's sabkhas. The National, 21 November 2010.","Alsharhan, A.S. and Kendall, C.G.St.C. (2019). Interpretations of Holocene Carbonate-Evaporites of Coastal and Inland Sabkhas of Abu Dhabi. In Sabkha Ecosystems, Springer, pp. 151–187."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-wathba-fossil-dunes","n":"Al Wathba Fossil Dunes","na":"الكثبان المتحجرة في الوثبة","c":"geological","s":"More than 1,700 cemented Ice Age dunes · protected area opened 2022","lat":24.19,"lng":54.71,"p":"Pleistocene cemented aeolian sand","dr":"About 120,000–150,000 years ago","el":"Over 1,700 dunes in 7 km²","ic":"dune","era":"D","t0":150000,"t1":7000,"te":"Dunes turned to stone","ta":"كثبان تحوّلت إلى حجر","se":"Cross-bedded sand cemented by calcium carbonate from rising groundwater, then carved by wind into 1,700 frozen dunes east of Abu Dhabi","sa":"رمال متقاطعة الطبقات لحمتها كربونات الكالسيوم من المياه الجوفية الصاعدة ثم نحتتها الرياح إلى 1,700 كثيب متحجر شرق أبوظبي","desc":"The Al Wathba Fossil Dunes Protected Area, 45 km east of Abu Dhabi and south of the Al Wathba Wetland Reserve, covers about 7 km² and contains more than 1,700 lightly cemented, cross-bedded sand outcrops. The Environment Agency – Abu Dhabi dates them to about 120,000–150,000 years ago: sand blew in from the exposed bed of the Arabian Gulf during dry glacial periods, rising groundwater then cemented it with calcium carbonate, and wind carved the hardened sand into its present shapes. Prof Thomas Steuber of Khalifa University, who studied the dunes, notes that generations of dunes formed in ice-age cycles between about 200,000 and 7,000 years ago and that wind will eventually destroy them. The site opened free to the public in 2022 as an IUCN category III geological reserve and has been nominated for UNESCO's Global Geoparks list.","sig":"The dunes record the Gulf's dry glacial sea floor and the climate swings of the last ice ages.","ex":"Environment Agency – Abu Dhabi (EAD); Thomas Steuber, Khalifa University of Science and Technology","disc":["More than 1,700 fossil dunes in 7 km²","Nominated for the UNESCO Global Geoparks list"],"refs":["Khaleej Times (2022). UAE: Al Wathba reserve with rare 120,000-year-old fossil dunes opens to public, 1 February 2022.","CNN / ABC17 News (2022). Abu Dhabi's fossil dunes, with Thomas Steuber of Khalifa University."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"dubai-desert-conservation-reserve","n":"Dubai Desert Conservation Reserve","na":"محمية دبي الصحراوية","c":"ecology","s":"225 km² national park · about 800 Arabian oryx","lat":24.827,"lng":55.641,"p":"Protected desert, national park","dr":"Protected since 2003","el":"225 km², about 5% of Dubai","ic":"gazelle","era":"N","t0":23,"t1":0,"te":"Oryx on five percent of Dubai","ta":"المها على خمسة بالمئة من دبي","se":"A fenced reserve of 225 km² where 70 reintroduced Arabian oryx grew to about 800, with gazelles, houbara and more than 560 recorded species","sa":"محمية مسوّرة بمساحة 225 كم² تكاثر فيها 70 مهاً عربياً أُعيد توطينها إلى نحو 800، مع الغزلان والحبارى وأكثر من 560 نوعاً مسجلاً","desc":"The Dubai Desert Conservation Reserve covers 225 km², almost 5% of Dubai's land area, about 40 km from the city. It began in 1999 around the 27 km² Al Maha resort and was proclaimed a national park and protected area in 2003, run under the Dubai Conservation Board with a team of 15 conservation officers; domestic livestock were removed in 2008. The reserve reintroduced 70 Arabian oryx and now holds about 800, the largest free-roaming herd in the UAE, with 171 relocated to other reserves, plus about 200 sand gazelles, 420 mountain gazelles and 2,800 reintroduced houbara bustards. Surveys record 18 mammal, 142 bird, 26 reptile, more than 300 arthropod and 74 native plant species across gravel plains, sand dunes and valley dunes.","sig":"It shows how a fenced desert reserve can rebuild a herd of a species once extinct in the wild.","ex":"Dubai Conservation Board; Emirates Group (over Dh28 million invested); visiting university researchers","disc":["Oryx grew from 70 to about 800 animals","More than 560 species recorded, up from about 150"],"refs":["Gulf News (2023). Video: A walk through the Dubai Desert Conservation Reserve.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Dubai Desert Conservation Reserve, citing The Telegraph (2024)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"ras-ghanada-reef","n":"Ras Ghanada Reef","na":"شعاب رأس غناضة المرجانية","c":"ecology","s":"Largest coral reef in the UAE · 55 km² protected area","lat":24.88,"lng":54.75,"p":"Living coral reef","dr":"Monitored since 2010","el":"About 40% of Arabian Gulf coral species","ic":"wave","era":"N","t0":20,"t1":0,"te":"The reef that survived the heat","ta":"الشعاب التي نجت من الحرارة","se":"A long reef strip near Ghantoot, the most extensive reef on the Gulf side of the UAE, home to turtles, dugongs, sea snakes and clownfish","sa":"شريط مرجاني طويل قرب غنتوت، أوسع شعاب على ساحل الخليج العربي في الإمارات، تعيش فيه السلاحف والأطوم وثعابين البحر وسمك المهرج","desc":"Ras Ghanada, near Ghantoot about 90 km north-east of Abu Dhabi city, is a long, narrow reef strip running parallel to the mainland for several kilometres. Dr John Burt of New York University Abu Dhabi calls it the most extensive reef on the Arabian Gulf side of the UAE, and EWS-WWF described it in 2007 as the best and healthiest reef in Abu Dhabi; Burt has monitored it with the Environment Agency – Abu Dhabi since 2010 using fish counts and seafloor photographs every three metres. The 1996–98 El Niño killed about 90% of Gulf corals, and the reef lies close to the breakwater of Khalifa Port, opened in 2012. The Ras Ghanadah Protected Area now covers 55 km² and holds about 40% of the coral species of the Arabian Gulf, with turtles, dugongs, sea snakes and clownfish.","sig":"It is the UAE's largest coral reef and a natural laboratory for corals living in the world's hottest sea.","ex":"Environment Agency – Abu Dhabi; John Burt, NYU Abu Dhabi marine biology laboratory; Emirates Wildlife Society–WWF","disc":["About 40% of Arabian Gulf coral species in one reef","Protected area of 55 km²"],"refs":["The National (2015). Guardians of Ras Ghanada: Abu Dhabi's most vibrant coral reef, 5 July 2015.","Experience Abu Dhabi (DCT). Marine Protected Areas in Abu Dhabi.","Ibrahim, M. (2024). An abundance of life thrives in Abu Dhabi's marine protected areas. Aletihad, 10 July 2024."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"khor-al-beidah","n":"Khor Al Beidah","na":"خور البيضاء","c":"ecology","s":"Tidal lagoon of Umm Al Quwain · Important Bird Area","lat":25.55,"lng":55.62,"p":"Intertidal lagoon, mangrove and sandflats","dr":"IBA since 2017","el":"Up to 4,000 lesser sand plovers in winter","ic":"bird","era":"N","t0":10,"t1":0,"te":"Where the crab plovers winter","ta":"حيث تشتو زقزاقات السرطان","se":"An unprotected tidal lagoon around Al Sinniyah Island that hosts 350–450 wintering crab plovers, endangered great knots and thousands of sand plovers","sa":"خور مدّي غير محمي حول جزيرة السينية يستضيف 350–450 زقزاق سرطان شاتياً وطيور القطقاط الكبير المهددة وآلاف الزقزاقات الرملية","desc":"Khor Al Beidah is a tidal wetland of mudflats, sandflats and mangrove in the Emirate of Umm Al Quwain, listed as an Important Bird Area by BirdLife International and the UAE Ministry of Climate Change and Environment in 2017. Counts from 2010–2015 recorded 350–450 wintering crab plovers, 1,700–4,000 lesser sand plovers, 850–2,000 greater sand plovers, 1,000–2,000 Kentish plovers, 210–310 western reef egrets and 60–88 endangered great knots. More than 420 bird species have been recorded in the region, but less than 10% of the site is covered by any protected designation and BirdLife rates the pressure as high, from biological resource use and likely residential and commercial development. Illegal cutting of mangroves has been reported.","sig":"It is one of the most important shorebird wintering lagoons on the UAE's Gulf coast and still lacks formal protection.","ex":"BirdLife International; UAE Ministry of Climate Change and Environment; Emirates Nature–WWF","disc":["350–450 crab plovers winter here","Less than 10% of the site has any protected status"],"refs":["BirdLife International (2017). Important Bird Area factsheet: Khor Al Beidah, United Arab Emirates.","Wikipedia contributors (2026). Khor Al Beidah, citing The National and the UAE Ministry of Climate Change and Environment."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"al-zorah-reserve","n":"Al Zorah Nature Reserve","na":"محمية الزوراء الطبيعية","c":"ecology","s":"Mangrove lagoon of Ajman · Ramsar wetland, protected since 2004","lat":25.43,"lng":55.49,"p":"Mangrove creek and lagoon","dr":"Protected since 2004; Ramsar 2018","el":"195 hectares, 108 animal species","ic":"mangrove","era":"N","t0":22,"t1":0,"te":"Flamingos at the creek mouth","ta":"طيور النحام عند مصبّ الخور","se":"A 195-hectare mangrove and lagoon reserve at the mouth of Ajman Creek with pink flamingos and over 50 other bird species, the UAE's seventh Ramsar site","sa":"محمية قرم وبحيرات بمساحة 195 هكتاراً عند مصب خور عجمان تزورها طيور النحام الوردي وأكثر من 50 نوعاً آخر من الطيور، وهي سابع موقع رامسار في الإمارات","desc":"Al Zorah Nature Reserve covers 195 hectares straddling the mouth of the one-kilometre-long Ajman Creek, with mangroves, sandy beaches, salt flats and lagoons. Legally protected since 2004, it was declared a Wetland of International Importance in 2018, the seventh Ramsar site in the UAE, with 108 recorded animal species including birds, fish, corals and molluscs. Large numbers of pink flamingos gather at certain times of year alongside more than 50 other bird species, and the mangroves are among the few remaining in the northern Emirates. On 24 November 2023, 6,600 mangrove saplings were planted over 0.9 hectares in a restoration project with Emirates Nature–WWF.","sig":"It is the main surviving mangrove wetland of Ajman and shows a small emirate protecting its creek amid urban growth.","ex":"Ajman Municipality and Planning Department; UAE Ministry of Climate Change and Environment; Emirates Nature–WWF","disc":["Seventh Ramsar site in the UAE","6,600 mangrove saplings planted in 2023"],"refs":["Gulf News (2018). Ajman wetland joins globally protected list, 1 November 2018.","Emirates Nature–WWF. Al Zorah, Ajman (emiratesnaturewwf.ae)."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"mangrove-national-park-abu-dhabi","n":"Mangrove National Park","na":"منتزه القرم الوطني","c":"ecology","s":"Eastern Mangroves of Abu Dhabi city · 10 km² of grey mangrove","lat":24.46,"lng":54.43,"p":"Avicennia marina forest","dr":"Restoration since the 1970s","el":"About 10 km² of mangrove","ic":"mangrove","era":"N","t0":50,"t1":0,"te":"A forest beside the skyline","ta":"غابة بجانب ناطحات السحاب","se":"Grey mangrove channels on the eastern edge of Abu Dhabi city, about 10 km² and some three-quarters of the UAE's mangrove forest, with flamingos, herons and turtles","sa":"قنوات القرم الرمادي على الطرف الشرقي لمدينة أبوظبي بمساحة نحو 10 كم²، أي نحو ثلاثة أرباع غابات القرم في الإمارات، مع طيور النحام والبلشون والسلاحف","desc":"The Mangrove National Park lies along the Eastern Mangroves corniche of Abu Dhabi city and covers about 10 km², described by Aletihad as about 75% of the UAE's total mangrove forest area and protected by the Environment Agency – Abu Dhabi. The forest is grey mangrove, Avicennia marina, locally called qurm, which grows 2–4 m tall in the intertidal zone; it absorbs carbon, purifies water and buffers tidal surges. Greater flamingos, western reef herons, crabs, turtles, foxes, dugongs and dolphins are recorded, and visitors explore by boardwalk, kayak and eco-boat. Sheikh Zayed began mangrove restoration in the 1970s; the UAE now has about 60 million mangrove trees over roughly 200 km² and aims for 100 million by 2030.","sig":"It is the largest urban mangrove forest in the Emirates and the heart of the country's mangrove-planting ambition.","ex":"Environment Agency – Abu Dhabi (EAD)","disc":["About 10 km² of mangrove next to the city","UAE target of 100 million mangrove trees by 2030"],"refs":["Ibrahim, M. (2024). An abundance of life thrives in Abu Dhabi's marine protected areas. Aletihad, 10 July 2024.","Gulf News (2026). Abu Dhabi's mangroves: A natural solution to climate change and coastal protection."],"conf":"","ev":"","an":"","val":""},{"id":"spec-hafeet-water","n":"The Leaning Mountain and the First Oasis","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":24.17,"lng":55.78,"p":"Hafit to Iron Age","dr":"c. 3200–300 BC (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between three records on this map. Jebel Hafeet is an asymmetric anticline of Eocene to Miocene marine limestone, its east limb overturned at about 70°, cut by faults that carry water from 2 km down to hot springs of about 40 °C at its foot. More than 500 Hafit-period tombs (3200–2600 BC) crown its foothills, and 20–30 km north, at Hili and Bidaa Bint Saud, lie the oldest oasis farming in south-eastern Arabia (c. 3000 BC) and Iron Age aflaj that are among the earliest known. The idea: the fractured, folded limestone of the mountain and the faults beneath it feed shallow groundwater along its flanks and the gravel fans to the north, and this water, not the mountain itself, is why people first built, farmed and dug here.","sig":"If true, the oldest monuments and the oldest irrigation of the Emirates would both be explained by one geological structure, and the aflaj of Al Ain would be read as an engineering answer to a fold in the rock.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Structure: Jebel Hafeet record describes faults beneath the fold carrying water from about 2 km depth to 40 °C springs at Green Mubazzarah. Monuments: about 500 Hafit tombs on the foothills of the same mountain, 3200–2600 BC (Jebel Hafeet Tombs record). Water use: Hili 8 farming c. 3000 BC with wheat, barley and date palm; Iron Age aflaj at Hili and Bidaa Bint Saud, 15–25 km north of Al Ain, dated before the Achaemenid Empire (Hili and Bidaa Bint Saud records). Distances: tombs 1–3 km from the fold axis; Hili about 25 km north; Bidaa Bint Saud about 35 km north.","an":"At Delphi, Greece, de Boer, Hale and Chanton (2001) showed that the sanctuary sits where two faults intersect, and that spring water and gases rising along the faults explain why that exact spot was chosen for the oracle; the geology selected the site before the builders did (Geology 29, 707–710).","val":"Map springs, seeps and shallow wells around Jebel Hafeet and along the line to Hili and Bidaa Bint Saud and compare their positions with the faults of the fold; analyse the water chemistry and isotopes of the falaj mother wells and the hot springs to see whether they share a deep, fault-fed source; date the earliest falaj channels and the tombs and test whether the oldest settlement and tomb clusters lie nearest the water-bearing structures.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the tombs were placed on the ridges for visibility and ancestral display, and the mountain mattered as a landmark, not a water source.","Rival explanation 2: the oasis water comes from the Hajar mountain front and the wadi gravels of Oman, and Jebel Hafeet contributes nothing measurable.","Rival explanation 3: the falaj was invented to tap any alluvial aquifer, and Al Ain is simply where the best-preserved early examples survived."],"refs":["Searle, M. P. & Ali, M. Y. (2009). Structural and tectonic evolution of the Jabal Sumeini–Al Ain–Buraimi region. GeoArabia 14(1).","Emirates Natural History Group (2011). The United Arab Emirates throughout the ages.","UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Cultural Sites of Al Ain (Hafit, Hili, Bidaa Bint Saud and Oases Areas), ref. 1343, inscribed 2011.","Al Tikriti, W.Y. (2002). The south-east Arabian origin of the falaj system. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 32: 117–138.","de Boer, J.Z., Hale, J.R. & Chanton, J. (2001). New evidence for the geological origins of the ancient Delphic oracle (Greece). Geology 29(8), 707–710."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did the Fold Bring the Water?","ta":"هل جلبت الطية الماء؟","se":"A folded mountain that pumps hot water from 2 km down, 500 tombs on its flanks, and the first farms and falaj of Arabia within a day’s walk: one structure may have fed them all. Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"جبل مطوي يدفع ماءً ساخنًا من عمق كيلومترين، و500 مدفن على سفوحه، وأول مزارع وأفلاج في الجزيرة العربية على مسافة يوم سيرًا: ربما غذّاها كلها تركيب واحد. لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"الجبل المائل والواحة الأولى","t0":5225,"t1":2325,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"hafeet-water","sty":"ai-hafeet-water","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of places on this map. The geological record of Jebel Hafeet describes faults feeding 40 °C springs at the foot of the fold. The archaeological records of the Hafit tombs, Hili and Bidaa Bint Saud describe the oldest monuments, farms and aflaj of the Emirates, all strung along the same mountain and the plain to its north.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق أماكن على هذه الخريطة. يصف السجل الجيولوجي لجبل حفيت صدوعًا تغذي ينابيع بحرارة 40 درجة عند سفح الطية. وتصف السجلات الأثرية لمدافن حفيت والهيلي وبدع بنت سعود أقدم المعالم والمزارع والأفلاج في الإمارات، وكلها منظومة على طول الجبل نفسه والسهل الذي شماله."},"lk":[["jebel-hafeet","The fold and the faults that carry water from 2 km down","الطية والصدوع التي تحمل الماء من عمق كيلومترين"],["jebel-hafeet-tombs","More than 500 Hafit tombs on the foothills, 3200–2600 BC","أكثر من 500 مدفن من فترة حفيت على السفوح، 3200–2600 ق.م"],["hili","The first oasis farming c. 3000 BC and early aflaj","أول زراعة واحات نحو 3000 ق.م وأفلاج مبكرة"],["bidaa-bint-saud","Iron Age falaj older than the Achaemenid Empire","فلج من العصر الحديدي أقدم من الإمبراطورية الأخمينية"]],"pred":{"en":"Springs, seeps and the mother wells of the earliest aflaj will line up with the faults of the Hafeet fold or the gravel fans it feeds, their water will carry a chemical signature of deep circulation, and the oldest tomb and settlement clusters will sit closest to these water-bearing lines.","ar":"ستصطف الينابيع والنزّات والآبار الأم لأقدم الأفلاج مع صدوع طية حفيت أو مراوح الحصى التي تغذيها، وسيحمل ماؤها بصمة كيميائية لدوران عميق، وستقع أقدم تجمعات المدافن والمستوطنات أقرب ما يكون إلى هذه الخطوط الحاملة للماء."},"test":{"en":"Map the springs and early falaj mother wells against the fold’s faults; compare the chemistry and isotopes of falaj water with the hot springs; date the earliest channels and tombs and check whether they cluster near the water.","ar":"رسم الينابيع والآبار الأم للأفلاج المبكرة على خريطة صدوع الطية، ومقارنة كيمياء ماء الأفلاج ونظائره مع الينابيع الساخنة، وتأريخ أقدم القنوات والمدافن والتحقق مما إذا كانت تتجمع قرب الماء."},"wrong":{"en":"If the falaj water matches the Hajar wadi gravels and shares nothing with the fault-fed springs, or if the tombs and farms are spread evenly with no relation to the water-bearing structures, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا تطابق ماء الأفلاج مع حصى أودية الحجر ولم يشترك في شيء مع الينابيع المغذاة بالصدوع، أو إذا توزعت المدافن والمزارع بالتساوي دون علاقة بالتراكيب الحاملة للماء، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-baynunah-sand","n":"The Old River in the Fossil Dunes","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":23.94,"lng":54.33,"p":"Late Miocene to Late Pleistocene","dr":"c. 150,000–7,000 years ago (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between two records on this map. The Baynunah Formation at Mleisa was laid down about seven million years ago by a braided river system with channels three metres deep, and it spreads across Al Dhafra under a thin skin of modern sand. About 100 km to the north-east, the Al Wathba Fossil Dunes are more than 1,700 cemented, cross-bedded dune outcrops dated to about 120,000–150,000 years ago; the accepted story is that their sand blew in from the exposed bed of the Arabian Gulf during dry glacial periods and was then cemented by rising groundwater. The idea: part of the sand in the fossil dunes was not Gulf-floor sediment at all, but Baynunah river sand picked up by the wind as it crossed the eroding Miocene outcrops of Al Dhafra, so that the dunes carry a measurable share of the old river inside them.","sig":"If true, the fossil dunes would record not only the glacial sea floor but the slow unmaking of the Miocene river landscape, and sand provenance would become a tool for dating when the Baynunah surface was stripped and when the wind blew from the south-west rather than the north-west.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Mleisa record: Baynunah Formation is river sand and mud with channels 3 m deep, exposed over Al Dhafra, west and south-west of the dunes. Al Wathba Fossil Dunes record: more than 1,700 outcrops in 7 km², dated c. 120,000–150,000 years ago, built from sand that blew in during dry glacial periods and was cemented by groundwater; generations of dunes formed in ice-age cycles between c. 200,000 and 7,000 years ago. Distance: about 100 km between the two records, along the line of the prevailing winds that build the dunes of Abu Dhabi. The abu-dhabi-coastal-sabkha record gives the carbonate, Gulf-derived end member for comparison.","an":"Garzanti, Vermeesch, Andò and colleagues (2013) traced the sand of the Arabian deserts to its sources with heavy minerals and detrital zircon ages, showing that the Rub’ al-Khali and the dunes of the Gulf coast are mixtures of sediment carried by ancient rivers from Arabia’s interior and by the Tigris–Euphrates system, and that each source leaves a fingerprint that survives recycling through many generations of dunes (Earth-Science Reviews 120, 1–19).","val":"Sample the cemented sand of several fossil-dune generations and the Baynunah river sands at Mleisa; compare grain size, heavy-mineral suites, carbonate versus quartz content and detrital zircon age spectra; use the Gulf-floor carbonate sand of the coastal sabkha as the second end member; estimate the mixing proportion in each dune generation and check whether it changes with age.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: all the dune sand came from the exposed Gulf floor, itself a mix of Tigris–Euphrates and local carbonate sediment, with no Baynunah contribution.","Rival explanation 2: any Baynunah-like fingerprint comes from the Rub’ al-Khali sands to the south, which share older sources with the Miocene river, so the signal cannot be tied to the Baynunah outcrops.","Rival explanation 3: groundwater cementation altered the mineralogy of the dunes so that provenance signals are no longer readable."],"refs":["Bibi, F., Kraatz, B., Hill, A. et al. (2012). Early evidence for complex social structure in Proboscidea from a late Miocene trackway site in the United Arab Emirates. Biology Letters (Royal Society).","Department of Culture and Tourism – Abu Dhabi. Mleisa Elephant Trackway; Baynunah Geological Formation Fossil Sites (abudhabiculture.ae).","Khaleej Times (2022). UAE: Al Wathba reserve with rare 120,000-year-old fossil dunes opens to public, 1 February 2022.","CNN / ABC17 News (2022). Abu Dhabi's fossil dunes, with Thomas Steuber of Khalifa University.","Evans, G. et al. (1969). Stratigraphy and geologic history of the sabkha, Abu Dhabi. Sedimentology 12.","Garzanti, E., Vermeesch, P., Andò, S., Vezzoli, G., Valagussa, M., Allen, K., Kadi, K.A. & Al-Juboury, A.I.A. (2013). Provenance and recycling of Arabian desert sand. Earth-Science Reviews 120, 1–19."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Is the Elephants’ River Inside the Dunes?","ta":"هل نهر الفيلة داخل الكثبان؟","se":"The sand of a river that elephants crossed seven million years ago lies open to the wind a hundred kilometres upwind of dunes that turned to stone in the ice ages; the dunes may be made partly of that river. Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"رمل نهرٍ عبرته الفيلة قبل سبعة ملايين سنة مكشوفٌ للريح على بعد مئة كيلومتر في اتجاه الريح من كثبان تحجرت في العصور الجليدية؛ وقد تكون الكثبان مصنوعة جزئيًا من ذلك النهر. لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"النهر القديم في الكثبان المتحجرة","t0":150000,"t1":7000,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"baynunah-sand","sty":"ai-baynunah-sand","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of places and ages on this map. The Mleisa record describes a seven-million-year-old river system exposed across Al Dhafra. The Al Wathba Fossil Dunes record describes sand blown in during the ice ages from the exposed Gulf floor, about 100 km downwind of the river outcrops. Nobody in either record asks whether the river is in the dunes.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق أماكن وأعمار على هذه الخريطة. يصف سجل مليسا نظامًا نهريًا عمره سبعة ملايين سنة مكشوفًا عبر الظفرة. ويصف سجل كثبان الوثبة المتحجرة رملًا هبّت به الريح في العصور الجليدية من قاع الخليج المكشوف، على بعد نحو 100 كيلومتر في اتجاه الريح من مكاشف النهر. ولا يسأل أي من السجلين عما إذا كان النهر داخل الكثبان."},"lk":[["mleisa-elephant-trackway","The Baynunah river sands, seven million years old, open to the wind","رمال نهر بينونة، عمرها سبعة ملايين سنة، مكشوفة للريح"],["al-wathba-fossil-dunes","Ice-age dunes, 120,000–150,000 years old, cemented into stone","كثبان من العصر الجليدي، عمرها 120,000–150,000 سنة، تحجرت"],["abu-dhabi-coastal-sabkha","The Gulf-floor carbonate sand: the rival source","رمل قاع الخليج الكربوناتي: المصدر المنافس"]],"pred":{"en":"The cemented sand of the fossil dunes will contain a measurable fraction of grains whose size, heavy minerals and zircon ages match the Baynunah river sands rather than the carbonate sand of the Gulf floor, and that fraction will be larger in generations formed when winds blew from the south-west.","ar":"سيحتوي الرمل المتحجر في الكثبان على نسبة قابلة للقياس من حبيبات تطابق في حجمها ومعادنها الثقيلة وأعمار الزركون فيها رمال نهر بينونة لا رمل قاع الخليج الكربوناتي، وستكون هذه النسبة أكبر في الأجيال التي تكوّنت حين هبّت الرياح من الجنوب الغربي."},"test":{"en":"Compare grain size, heavy minerals, carbonate content and detrital zircon ages of the fossil-dune sand with the Baynunah sands at Mleisa and the Gulf-floor sand of the sabkha, generation by generation.","ar":"مقارنة حجم الحبيبات والمعادن الثقيلة ومحتوى الكربونات وأعمار الزركون في رمل الكثبان المتحجرة مع رمال بينونة في مليسا ورمل قاع الخليج في السبخة، جيلًا بعد جيل."},"wrong":{"en":"If every dune generation matches the Gulf-floor and Tigris–Euphrates fingerprint with no Baynunah component, or if the Baynunah signal cannot be separated from the Rub’ al-Khali sands, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا طابق كل جيل من الكثبان بصمة قاع الخليج ودجلة والفرات دون أي مكوّن من بينونة، أو إذا تعذّر فصل إشارة بينونة عن رمال الربع الخالي، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-beidah-pearls","n":"The Lagoon That Fed the Pearling Town","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":25.565,"lng":55.61,"p":"Late antique to early Islamic","dr":"c. AD 580–750 (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between two records on this map. Al Sinniyah Island, in the Umm Al Quwain lagoon, held the oldest pearling town yet found in the Arabian Gulf, about 12 hectares of houses occupied for about 200 years from the late 6th or early 7th century to the mid-8th century AD, with oyster-shell middens of possibly millions of shells. The same lagoon, Khor Al Beidah, is today a tidal wetland of mudflats, sandflats and mangrove that feeds 350–450 wintering crab plovers and thousands of sand plovers. The idea: the town was placed where it was because the sheltered lagoon itself was a working fishery, and its middens are built mainly from oysters, fish and shellfish gathered inside the lagoon rather than from pearl oysters brought back from offshore banks, so that the modern bird wetland and the ancient town are two readings of one productive system.","sig":"If true, the first pearling town of the Gulf would be understood as a lagoon economy as much as an offshore one, and the unprotected Khor Al Beidah would gain a heritage value of thirteen centuries on top of its value for birds.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Al Sinniyah record: town of several thousand people, late 6th/early 7th to mid-8th century AD, houses of local beach rock, pearls, a diver’s weight, and oyster-shell middens of possibly millions of shells; merchants’ courtyard houses beside fishermen’s one- or two-room houses. Khor Al Beidah record: mudflats, sandflats and mangrove supporting 350–450 crab plovers, 1,700–4,000 lesser sand plovers and 850–2,000 greater sand plovers in 2010–2015 counts, with less than 10% of the site protected. Distance: the island sits inside the lagoon; the two records are about 3 km apart.","an":"In Denmark, Søren H. Andersen (2000) surveyed the Stone Age shell middens of the Ertebølle culture and showed that their composition tracks the fjords beside them: layers dominated by oysters give way to cockles and mussels as the local waters changed, so that each midden is a record of the fishery at its doorstep (Proceedings of the Prehistoric Society 66, 361–384).","val":"Sample the Al Sinniyah middens by layer and identify the species: pearl oysters (Pinctada) from offshore banks versus edible oysters, clams and fish bones from lagoon habitats; measure shell sizes and growth lines to see where and in which season they were gathered; compare with the present species of Khor Al Beidah; core the lagoon sediments to test whether the mudflats and mangroves already existed in the 7th century.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the middens are almost entirely pearl oyster from the offshore banks, and the lagoon served only as a sheltered harbour.","Rival explanation 2: the lagoon of the 7th century was deeper and more open than today’s mudflats, so the modern wetland is not the one the town knew.","Rival explanation 3: the town’s food came mainly by trade, as the 12% of imported Indian pottery suggests, and local gathering was marginal."],"refs":["Power, T., Degli Esposti, M., Hoyland, R. et al. (2024). Excavations at a late antique to early Islamic pearling town and monastery on Sīnīya Island, Umm al-Quwain. Proceedings of the Seminar for Arabian Studies 53: 211–229.","Dennehy, J. (2023). Oldest pearling town in the Arabian Gulf discovered in Umm Al Quwain. The National, 20 March 2023.","BirdLife International (2017). Important Bird Area factsheet: Khor Al Beidah, United Arab Emirates.","Andersen, S.H. (2000). \"Køkkenmøddinger\" (shell middens) in Denmark: a survey. Proceedings of the Prehistoric Society 66, 361–384."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did the Town Live off Its Own Lagoon?","ta":"هل عاشت البلدة من خورها؟","se":"The lagoon that feeds thousands of plovers today may be the same larder that fed a town of several thousand people and millions of shells thirteen centuries ago. Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"الخور الذي يطعم آلاف الزقزاقات اليوم قد يكون المخزن نفسه الذي أطعم بلدة من عدة آلاف من الناس وملايين الأصداف قبل ثلاثة عشر قرنًا. لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"الخور الذي أطعم بلدة اللؤلؤ","t0":1450,"t1":1275,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"beidah-pearls","sty":"ai-beidah-pearls","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of places on this map. The Al Sinniyah record describes middens of possibly millions of shells on an island inside the Umm Al Quwain lagoon. The Khor Al Beidah record describes the same lagoon as a wetland rich enough to feed thousands of shorebirds, with less than 10% of it protected. One lagoon, two records, no link between them yet.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق أماكن على هذه الخريطة. يصف سجل السينية أكوامًا من الأصداف ربما بلغت الملايين على جزيرة داخل خور أم القيوين. ويصف سجل خور البيضاء الخور نفسه أرضًا رطبة غنية بما يكفي لإطعام آلاف الطيور الشاطئية، مع حماية أقل من 10% منه. خور واحد، وسجلان، ولا رابط بينهما بعد."},"lk":[["al-sinniyah-island","The pearling town and its middens of millions of shells","بلدة اللؤلؤ وأكوامها من ملايين الأصداف"],["khor-al-beidah","The lagoon that feeds thousands of shorebirds today","الخور الذي يطعم آلاف الطيور الشاطئية اليوم"]],"pred":{"en":"The middens will contain, beside pearl oysters, large quantities of lagoon species such as edible oysters, clams and the bones of mudflat fish, with growth lines showing year-round gathering, and lagoon cores will show mudflats and mangrove already present in the 7th century.","ar":"ستحتوي الأكوام، إلى جانب محار اللؤلؤ، على كميات كبيرة من أنواع الخور كالمحار الصالح للأكل والبطلينوس وعظام أسماك الطين، مع خطوط نمو تدل على جمع طوال العام، وستُظهر عينات الخور اللبية وجود الطين والقرم منذ القرن السابع."},"test":{"en":"Identify the species in the middens layer by layer and compare them with the lagoon today; read shell growth lines for season and habitat; core the lagoon to date its mudflats and mangroves.","ar":"تحديد الأنواع في الأكوام طبقة بعد طبقة ومقارنتها بأنواع الخور اليوم، وقراءة خطوط نمو الأصداف لمعرفة الموسم والموطن، وأخذ عينات لبية من الخور لتأريخ طينه وقرمه."},"wrong":{"en":"If the middens are almost pure pearl oyster from offshore banks, or if the cores show that the lagoon was open water with no mudflats in the 7th century, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا كانت الأكوام محار لؤلؤ خالصًا تقريبًا من المغاصات البعيدة، أو إذا أظهرت العينات أن الخور كان ماءً مفتوحًا بلا طين في القرن السابع، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-ae-faya-ridge","n":"The Ridge People Kept Coming Back To","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":25.07,"lng":55.82,"p":"Palaeolithic to Neolithic","dr":"c. 210,000–6,000 years ago (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between four records on this map. Around Jebel Faya in central Sharjah, 18 distinct layers of human activity span more than 210,000 years, with handaxes, blades and points about 125,000 years old; the site is seen as a key place for how early humans crossed and survived Arabia through its wet and dry phases. A few kilometres to the north, Fossil Rock and the ridge of Jebel Maleihah are marine limestone laid down from the Late Cretaceous to the Paleocene on top of the Samail Ophiolite. About 12 km to the south, the limestone ridge of Jebel Buhais, with fossils at least 93 million years old, runs almost continuously from Madam to Mleiha and carries a Neolithic cemetery of about 5000–4000 BC whose burials are read as those of herders who came inland for the winter. The idea: the line of fractured limestone ridges resting on ophiolite works as a natural store of water and shelter, so that rain soaking into the limestone seeps out again along the ridge feet; this, and not only the wet phases, is why people returned to Faya again and again and why herders chose Buhais for their winters.","sig":"If true, the long record of Faya would be explained by a geological structure that it shares with Fossil Rock and Jebel Buhais, and the whole ridge line from Madam to Mleiha would be a single place of refuge to survey for further sites of every age.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Faya Palaeolandscape record: 18 layers of human activity spanning more than 210,000 years; stone tools about 125,000 years old (2011); a landscape of dunes, rocky outcrops and fossil remnants; World Heritage 2025. Fossil Rock record: a few kilometres north of Jebel Faya; marine limestone, Late Cretaceous to early–mid Paleocene, on top of the Samail Ophiolite; Jebel Faya 412 m. Buhais Geology Park record: limestone ridge with fossils at least 93 million years old, running almost continuously from Madam to Mleiha. Jebel Buhais record: outcrop rising to about 340 m on the same ridge; cemetery BHS 18 dated about 5000–4000 BC with about 600 individuals, read as herders who came inland for the winter. Distances: Faya to Fossil Rock about 3 km; Faya to Jebel Buhais about 12 km.","an":"Breeze, Groucutt, Drake and colleagues (2016) mapped ancient rivers, lakes and wetlands across the Middle East from satellite data and dated deposits, and argued that in wet phases these formed corridors of fresh water along which early humans could move and survive; where water was found, the archaeology followed (Quaternary Science Reviews 144, 155–185).","val":"Survey the feet of the Faya, Maleihah and Buhais ridges for old spring and seep deposits, such as spring carbonates, and date them; compare their ages with the 18 occupation layers at Faya and the Neolithic cemetery at Buhais; map the contact between the limestone and the ophiolite beneath and test whether shallow groundwater collects along it today; check whether occupation at Faya continued into dry phases when the open desert around it was empty.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: people came only in wet phases, when the whole landscape held water, and the ridges played no special part.","Rival explanation 2: the ridges mattered for shelter, stone or as landmarks on a route, not for water.","Rival explanation 3: the long sequence at Faya is a matter of preservation: sediment trapped at the foot of the mountain kept the layers, while camps in the open desert were blown away."],"refs":["Current World Archaeology (2025). Faya Palaeolandscape becomes only site in the Arab world to be awarded UNESCO World Heritage status in 2025.","UNESCO World Heritage Centre. Faya Palaeolandscape, inscribed 2025.","NASA Earth Observatory (2025). Digging Back in Time in the UAE (Landsat 8 image of 23 October 2025).","Sharjah Environment and Protected Areas Authority. Buhais Geology Park.","Uerpmann, H.-P., Uerpmann, M. & Jasim, S. A. (2006). Funeral Monuments and Human Remains from Jebel al-Buhais. Department of Culture and Information, Sharjah.","Breeze, P.S., Groucutt, H.S., Drake, N.A., White, T.S., Jennings, R.P. & Petraglia, M.D. (2016). Palaeohydrological corridors for hominin dispersals in the Middle East ∼250–70,000 years ago. Quaternary Science Reviews 144, 155–185."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did the Limestone Ridges Make Faya a Refuge?","ta":"هل جعلت الحواف الجيرية الفاية ملاذًا؟","se":"People kept returning to Faya for 210,000 years, and herders wintered on the next ridge 6,000 years ago. Both sit on limestone ridges lying over ancient ocean crust. Did the rock hold the water that held the people? Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"ظلّ الناس يعودون إلى الفاية 210,000 عام، وقضى الرعاة الشتاء على الحافة المجاورة قبل 6,000 عام. وكلاهما على حواف جيرية فوق قشرة محيطية قديمة. فهل حبس الصخر الماء الذي أبقى الناس؟ لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"الحافة التي ظلّ الناس يعودون إليها","t0":210000,"t1":6025,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"aeridge","sty":"ai-aeridge","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of places on this map. The Faya record describes 18 layers of people over 210,000 years. The Fossil Rock and Buhais Geology Park records describe marine limestone ridges over the Samail Ophiolite, running from Madam to Mleiha. The Jebel Buhais record puts Neolithic herders’ winter burials on the same ridge line.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق أماكن على هذه الخريطة. يصف سجل الفاية 18 طبقة من البشر على مدى 210,000 عام. ويصف سجلا جبل الفوسيل وحديقة البحيص الجيولوجية حوافَّ من الحجر الجيري البحري فوق أفيوليت سمائل، تمتد من مدام إلى مليحة. ويضع سجل جبل البحيص مدافن الرعاة الشتوية من العصر الحجري الحديث على خط الحواف نفسه."},"lk":[["faya-palaeolandscape","18 layers of human activity over 210,000 years","18 طبقة من النشاط البشري على مدى 210,000 عام"],["fossil-rock","Marine limestone resting on the Samail Ophiolite","حجر جيري بحري يرقد فوق أفيوليت سمائل"],["buhais-geology-park","The limestone ridge from Madam to Mleiha","الحافة الجيرية من مدام إلى مليحة"],["jebel-buhais","Winter burials of Neolithic herders, 5000–4000 BC","مدافن شتوية لرعاة العصر الحجري الحديث، 5000–4000 ق.م"]],"pred":{"en":"Old spring and seep deposits will be found along the feet of the ridges, near the contact with the ophiolite, and their ages will overlap with occupation layers at Faya, including layers from dry phases when the surrounding desert shows no other sites.","ar":"ستُكتشف رواسب ينابيع ونزّات قديمة على امتداد أقدام الحواف، قرب التماس مع الأفيوليت، وستتداخل أعمارها مع طبقات الاستيطان في الفاية، ومنها طبقات من مراحل جافة لا تُظهر فيها الصحراء المحيطة أي مواقع أخرى."},"test":{"en":"Search the ridge feet for old spring deposits and date them; map the limestone–ophiolite contact and its groundwater; compare these with the dated layers at Faya and the cemetery at Buhais.","ar":"البحث عند أقدام الحواف عن رواسب ينابيع قديمة وتأريخها، ورسم خريطة للتماس بين الحجر الجيري والأفيوليت ومياهه الجوفية، ومقارنة ذلك بطبقات الفاية المؤرخة ومقبرة البحيص."},"wrong":{"en":"If no spring deposits are found along the ridges, or if their ages never match the occupation layers, or if Faya was used only in wet phases like the open desert, the idea fails.","ar":"إذا لم يُعثر على رواسب ينابيع على امتداد الحواف، أو لم تتطابق أعمارها أبدًا مع طبقات الاستيطان، أو إذا لم تُستخدم الفاية إلا في المراحل الرطبة مثل الصحراء المفتوحة، تسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-ae-old-mangroves","n":"Old Trees, Rare Birds","c":"speculative","s":"Ecology hypothesis","lat":24.9981,"lng":56.3625,"p":"Living today","dr":"c. 1970s–today (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between three records on this map. On the Gulf of Oman coast, Khor Kalba holds the tallest and most extensive mature mangrove woodland of grey mangrove, Avicennia marina, in the region; it is described as a relict forest, and it is almost the only breeding place of the Kalba white-collared kingfisher, an endemic subspecies, and one of only two regional breeding sites of Sykes’s warbler. On the Arabian Gulf coast, the Mangrove National Park of Abu Dhabi covers about 10 km² of the same species, 2–4 m tall, in forests restored since the 1970s; Al Zorah in Ajman protects 195 hectares with some of the few mangroves left in the northern Emirates, and 6,600 saplings were planted there in 2023. The idea: what makes Kalba special for its rare birds is not the size of its forest but its age and height, so that the younger forests on the Arabian Gulf coast will not hold these birds until they grow a structure like Kalba’s.","sig":"If true, mangrove planting in the Emirates should be measured not only in trees and square kilometres but in the decades needed to grow old forest, and Kalba’s mature woodland would be irreplaceable within a human lifetime.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Khor Kalba record: 1,494 ha, Ramsar 10 March 2013; tallest and most extensive mature mangrove woodland in the biogeographic region; Kalba white-collared kingfisher (Todiramphus chloris kalbaensis) breeds almost exclusively here; Sykes’s warbler has one of its two regional breeding sites here; hawksbill and green turtles feed in the creeks. Mangrove National Park record: about 10 km² of Avicennia marina, 2–4 m tall; restoration begun in the 1970s; flamingos, western reef herons, crabs, turtles, dugongs and dolphins recorded. Al Zorah record: 195 ha, protected since 2004, Ramsar 2018; among the few remaining mangroves of the northern Emirates; 108 animal species and more than 50 bird species; 6,600 saplings planted over 0.9 ha on 24 November 2023. Distances: Kalba to Al Zorah about 100 km, to Abu Dhabi about 200 km, across the Hajar Mountains.","an":"In Oregon, Forsman, Meslow and Wight (1984) studied the northern spotted owl and found it closely tied to old-growth conifer forest; young managed stands did not replace it, so the owl’s future depended on the age of the forest, not just its area (Wildlife Monographs 87).","val":"Lay out matching survey plots in Khor Kalba, Al Zorah and the Mangrove National Park; measure tree height, trunk thickness and stand age in each; map the nests and territories of the Kalba kingfisher and Sykes’s warbler at Kalba against the same measures; survey the Arabian Gulf forests for both species; compare water temperature, salinity and tide at the three sites to separate the effect of forest age from the effect of the sea.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the birds are held at Kalba by the Hajar Mountains and the distance between the two coasts, not by the trees; they simply cannot reach the western forests.","Rival explanation 2: the hotter, saltier water of the Arabian Gulf keeps its mangroves short whatever their age, so the western forests can never grow like Kalba.","Rival explanation 3: the birds depend on Kalba’s other habitats, such as its salt marsh, tidal channels and Acacia woodland, rather than on the age of the mangroves."],"refs":["Ramsar Sites Information Service. Mangrove and Al Hafeya Protected Area in Khor Kalba (site 2125), designated 2013.","Ibrahim, M. (2024). An abundance of life thrives in Abu Dhabi's marine protected areas. Aletihad, 10 July 2024.","Gulf News (2018). Ajman wetland joins globally protected list, 1 November 2018.","Emirates Nature–WWF. Al Zorah, Ajman (emiratesnaturewwf.ae).","Forsman, E.D., Meslow, E.C. & Wight, H.M. (1984). Distribution and biology of the spotted owl in Oregon. Wildlife Monographs 87, 3–64."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Does a Mangrove Need to Grow Old Before Its Rarest Birds Arrive?","ta":"هل يجب أن يشيخ القرم قبل أن تأتيه أندر طيوره؟","se":"The Emirates are planting millions of mangroves, and Abu Dhabi alone has about 10 km² of them. Yet a kingfisher found nowhere else breeds almost only in the old, tall trees of Khor Kalba. Is age the thing that cannot be planted? Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"تزرع الإمارات ملايين أشجار القرم، وفي أبوظبي وحدها نحو 10 كيلومترات مربعة منها. ومع ذلك فإن رفرافًا لا يوجد في أي مكان آخر يتكاثر تقريبًا في أشجار خور كلباء العتيقة الطويلة وحدها. فهل العمر هو ما لا يمكن زرعه؟ لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"أشجار عتيقة وطيور نادرة","t0":56,"t1":0,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"aemangrove","sty":"ai-aemangrove","spot":{"en":"Born from a contrast between records on this map. The Khor Kalba record describes the tallest, most mature mangroves of the region and two rare breeding birds. The Mangrove National Park and Al Zorah records describe younger, restored or newly planted forests of the same tree on the Arabian Gulf coast, restored and planted since the 1970s, without those birds.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تباين بين سجلات على هذه الخريطة. يصف سجل خور كلباء أطول أشجار القرم في المنطقة وأكثرها نضجًا وطائرين نادرين يتكاثران فيها. ويصف سجلا منتزه القرم الوطني والزوراء غابات أحدث عمرًا من الشجرة نفسها على ساحل الخليج العربي، أُعيد تأهيلها وزُرعت منذ السبعينيات، دون ذينك الطائرين."},"lk":[["khor-kalba","The tallest mature mangroves and the endemic Kalba kingfisher","أطول أشجار القرم الناضجة والرفراف الكلباوي المستوطن"],["mangrove-national-park-abu-dhabi","About 10 km² of restored mangrove, 2–4 m tall","نحو 10 كيلومترات مربعة من القرم المستعاد، بطول 2–4 أمتار"],["al-zorah-reserve","A small mangrove creek with new plantings in 2023","خور صغير من القرم بغراس جديدة عام 2023"]],"pred":{"en":"At Kalba the kingfisher and Sykes’s warbler will breed mainly in the tallest, oldest stands; the forests of Abu Dhabi and Al Zorah will be measurably younger and shorter and will lack both birds; and as the oldest restored stands near Kalba’s structure, they will be the first places outside Kalba where these birds are found.","ar":"سيتكاثر الرفراف ودخلة سايكس في كلباء غالبًا في أطول الأشجار وأقدمها، وستكون غابات أبوظبي والزوراء أصغر عمرًا وأقصر على نحو قابل للقياس وخالية من الطائرين، وحين تقترب أقدم المساحات المستعادة من بنية كلباء ستكون أول الأماكن خارجها التي يُعثر فيها على هذين الطائرين."},"test":{"en":"Measure tree height, trunk thickness and age in plots at all three sites; map the breeding birds at Kalba against these measures; survey the western forests for both species; compare the sea conditions at each site.","ar":"قياس طول الأشجار وسماكة جذوعها وأعمارها في مربعات مسح في المواقع الثلاثة، ورسم خريطة للطيور المتكاثرة في كلباء مقابل هذه القياسات، ومسح الغابات الغربية بحثًا عن الطائرين، ومقارنة ظروف البحر في كل موقع."},"wrong":{"en":"If the Kalba birds breed as readily in young or short stands as in old ones, or if western forests that already match Kalba’s structure still lack them, age is not the key and the idea fails.","ar":"إذا تكاثرت طيور كلباء في الأشجار الفتية أو القصيرة بالسهولة نفسها التي تتكاثر بها في العتيقة، أو إذا خلت منها غابات غربية تطابق بنية كلباء أصلًا، فالعمر ليس المفتاح وتسقط الفكرة."}}},{"id":"spec-ae-jais-tomb-stones","n":"Stones from the Mountain for the Dead","c":"speculative","s":"Cross-category hypothesis","lat":25.88,"lng":56.07,"p":"Umm an-Nar to Wadi Suq","dr":"c. 2600–1300 BC (test window)","el":"","desc":"A connection between three records on this map. At Shimal, about 8 km north-east of Ras Al Khaimah town, a cemetery of about 250 tombs includes the Umm an-Nar tomb Sh 222, 11.3 m across with about 300 burials (c. 2200–2000 BC), and Wadi Suq tombs such as Sh 99, 16 m long, and Sh 102, of the 15th–13th centuries BC. At Dhayah, about 8 km further on, twelve Wadi Suq tombs of c. 2000–1300 BC lie at the base of a hill between the date gardens and the Jebel Jais mountains. Jebel Jais itself, the highest peak of the Emirates, exposes marine and terrestrial sedimentary rocks with fossils, and the Hajar range holds ophiolites. The idea: the builders of these monumental tombs did not simply pick up the nearest boulders, but chose particular kinds of rock from the mountain front, and the choice changed between the Umm an-Nar and the Wadi Suq periods.","sig":"If true, the tombs of Shimal and Dhayah would record not only how people buried their dead but how they knew and used the rocks of the Hajar Mountains, and a change in stone could mark a change in who built them and why.","ex":"","conf":"Untested AI speculation (connection between published records)","ev":"Shimal record: about 250 tombs of several types, settled for at least 4,500 years; Umm an-Nar tomb Sh 222, 11.3 m across, about 300 burials, c. 2200–2000 BC; Wadi Suq tombs Sh 99 (16 m long, at least 66 individuals) and Sh 102 (about 140 individuals, 15th–13th centuries BC); soft-stone vessels among the finds. Dhayah Fort record: hill about 70 m high between the date gardens and the Jebel Jais mountains; Late Bronze Age settlement c. 1600–1300 BC; twelve Wadi Suq tombs of c. 2000–1300 BC at its base, four excavated. Jebel Jais record: summit 1,934 m; marine and terrestrial sedimentary rocks with fossils; Hajar range with ophiolites; formations more than 70 million years old. Distances: Shimal to Dhayah about 8 km; both within about 9–17 km of the summit of Jebel Jais.","an":"Parker Pearson and colleagues (2015) showed that some of the bluestones of Stonehenge came from Craig Rhos-y-felin in the Preseli Hills of west Wales, by matching the rock of the monument to particular outcrops and finding traces of Neolithic quarrying there; the builders had chosen a specific rock from a specific place (Antiquity 89, 1331–1352).","val":"Record the rock type of every visible stone in a sample of Umm an-Nar and Wadi Suq tombs at Shimal and Dhayah; count the rock types in the wadi gravels and slopes next to each tomb; compare the two sets; trace any rock types that are over-represented in the tombs to outcrops on the Jais mountain front through thin sections and chemistry; compare the choices of the two periods.","disc":["Rival explanation 1: the builders used whatever boulders lay nearest, and the tombs simply mirror the local gravels.","Rival explanation 2: stones were taken from older tombs and reused, so any pattern of choice has been blurred.","Rival explanation 3: stones were chosen for their size and shape, not their rock type, and any match to a source is a by-product."],"refs":["Emirates Natural History Group. Shimal Archaeological Site (enhg.org).","Hellyer, P. and Potts, D. (2012). Fifty Years of Emirates Archaeology. Abu Dhabi, pp. 216–221.","Ras Al Khaimah Department of Antiquities and Museums. Dhayah Fort (rakheritage.rak.ae).","Visit Ras Al Khaimah. The ancient story beneath the slopes: Jebel Jais history and geology explained.","Parker Pearson, M., Bevins, R., Ixer, R. et al. (2015). Craig Rhos-y-felin: a Welsh bluestone megalith quarry for Stonehenge. Antiquity 89(348), 1331–1352."],"ic":"spark","era":"?","te":"Did the Tomb Builders Choose Their Stone from Jebel Jais?","ta":"هل اختار بناة المدافن حجارتهم من جبل جيس؟","se":"The great tombs of Shimal and Dhayah stand at the foot of Jebel Jais, a mountain of sea-floor rock in a range that holds ancient ocean crust. Did their builders pick up any stone, or choose stones from the mountain on purpose? Nobody has tested this yet.","sa":"تقف مدافن شمل وضاية الكبيرة عند سفح جبل جيس، جبلٍ من صخور قاع البحر في سلسلة تضم قشرة محيطية قديمة. فهل التقط بناتها أي حجر، أم اختاروا حجارة الجبل عن قصد؟ لم يختبر أحد ذلك بعد.","na":"حجارة من الجبل للموتى","t0":4625,"t1":3275,"spec":{"bas":"link","key":"aejais","sty":"ai-aejais","spot":{"en":"Born from a coincidence of places on this map. The Shimal and Dhayah records describe monumental Umm an-Nar and Wadi Suq tombs a few kilometres apart at the mountain foot. The Jebel Jais record describes the many kinds of rock that the same mountains offer. Nobody in these records asks which rocks the builders used.","ar":"وُلدت الفكرة من تطابق أماكن على هذه الخريطة. يصف سجلا شمل وضاية مدافن ضخمة من فترتي أم النار ووادي سوق تفصلها كيلومترات قليلة عند سفح الجبل. ويصف سجل جبل جيس أنواع الصخر الكثيرة التي تقدّمها الجبال نفسها. ولا يسأل أي من هذه السجلات أيّ الصخور استعمل البناؤون."},"lk":[["shimal-tombs","About 250 tombs, from Umm an-Nar Sh 222 to Wadi Suq Sh 99 and Sh 102","نحو 250 مدفنًا، من مدفن أم النار Sh 222 إلى مدفني وادي سوق Sh 99 وSh 102"],["dhayah-fort","Twelve Wadi Suq tombs at the foot of the hill","اثنا عشر مدفنًا من فترة وادي سوق عند سفح التل"],["jebel-jais","The mountain front of sedimentary rocks and ophiolite","واجهة الجبل من الصخور الرسوبية والأفيوليت"]],"pred":{"en":"The tombs will contain a narrower or different mix of rock types than the gravels beside them, some of these rocks will trace to particular outcrops on the Jais mountain front, and Umm an-Nar and Wadi Suq tombs will show different choices.","ar":"ستحتوي المدافن على خليط من أنواع الصخر أضيق من الحصى المجاور لها أو مختلف عنه، وسيُردّ بعض هذه الصخور إلى مكاشف بعينها على واجهة جبل جيس، وستُظهر مدافن أم النار ووادي سوق اختيارات مختلفة."},"test":{"en":"Count rock types in the tomb walls and in the nearby gravels; trace over-represented rocks to outcrops by thin sections and chemistry; compare the two periods.","ar":"عدّ أنواع الصخور في جدران المدافن وفي الحصى القريب، وردّ الصخور الزائدة التمثيل إلى مكاشفها بالشرائح الرقيقة والتحليل الكيميائي، ومقارنة الفترتين."},"wrong":{"en":"If the tomb stones match the nearest gravels in the same proportions, and the Umm an-Nar and Wadi Suq tombs use the same mix, there was no choice and the idea fails.","ar":"إذا طابقت حجارة المدافن أقرب الحصى بالنسب نفسها، واستعملت مدافن أم النار ووادي سوق الخليط نفسه، فلم يكن هناك اختيار وتسقط الفكرة."}}}],"stops":[]})}catch(e){console.error("D",String(e).slice(0,80))}
